true or false is \"$8$ is a negative number.\" (This sentence is false.)\n\nAll the other sentences are neither true nor false." 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186059794 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351430 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186059817 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351403 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186059822 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351404 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186059822 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351405 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186059822 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351406 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186064269 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351313 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186064293 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351490 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186064293 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351491 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186064968 1068 #edb/ref 17592186063833 13194139533430 17592186045585 false] [17592186064968 1068 #edb/ref 17592186351017 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186064968 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186064968 1071 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration $a(t) = 2e^{2t}$, where $t \\ge 0$ is the time. If the particle has position $x = e^{2}$ and velocity $v = e^{2}$ when $t = 1$, calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t > 0$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533430 17592186045585 false] [17592186064968 1071 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration $a(t)=2{e}^{2t}$, where $t\\ge 0$ is the time. If the particle has position $x={e}^{2}$ and velocity $v={e}^{2}$ when $t=1$, calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t>0.$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186064968 1074 "We start by integrating the acceleration to get the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int 2{e}^{2t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={e}^{2t}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(1)={e}^{2}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2} & ={e}^{2(1)}+C \\\\ {e}^{2} & ={e}^{2}+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the velocity is given by $v(t)={e}^{2t}.$ \n\nNow, we integrate the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int {e}^{2t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}+K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $K,$ we use the fact that $x(1)={e}^{2}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2(1)}+K \\\\ {e}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2}+K \\\\ K & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186068753 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351467 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186068753 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351468 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186068754 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351496 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186068754 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351503 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186068807 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351482 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186068807 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351483 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186068807 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351484 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186068810 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351489 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186068847 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351485 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186069537 1068 #edb/ref 17592186068362 13194139533430 17592186045585 false] [17592186069537 1068 #edb/ref 17592186351050 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186069537 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186069537 1071 "$\\displaystyle \\int(3x + 1)/((x + 1)^{3})dx =$\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533430 17592186045585 false] [17592186069537 1071 "$\\int \\frac{3x+1}{(x+1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186069537 1074 "First, we must convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3x+1}{(x+1)^{3}}=\\frac{A}{x+1}+\\frac{B}{(x+1)^{2}}+\\frac{C}{(x+1)^{3}}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x+1)^{3}$ and get\n\n$$\n3x+1=A(x+1)^{2}+B(x+1)+C.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-1,$ we can eliminate $A$ and $B$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(-1)+1 & =A(-1+1)^{2}+B(-1+1)+C \\\\ -2 & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known value of $C,$ we get an equation relating $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(0)+1 & =A(0+1)^{2}+B(0+1)+C \\\\ 1 & =A+B-2 \\\\ A+B & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, plugging in $x=-2$ and substituting the known value of $C,$ we get an equation relating $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(-2)+1 & =A(-2+1)^{2}+B(-2+1)+C \\\\ -5 & =A-B-2 \\\\ A-B & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the equations $A-B=-3$ and $A+B=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2A & =-3+3 \\\\ 2A & =0 \\\\ A & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting back into $A+B=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+B & =3 \\\\ B & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum\n\n$$\n\\frac{3x+1}{(x+1)^{3}}=\\frac{3}{(x+1)^{2}}-\\frac{2}{(x+1)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3x+1}{(x+1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{3}{(x+1)^{2}}-\\frac{2}{(x+1)^{3}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int \\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int \\frac{1}{(x+1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{x+1}+\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}+K,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is an integral constant." 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186069538 1068 #edb/ref 17592186068368 13194139533430 17592186045585 false] [17592186069538 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350954 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186069538 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186069538 1071 "Find the general solution to the differential equation $y^{′} - \\sqrt{y} = 2x\\sqrt{y}$ for $y \\ne 0$." 13194139533430 17592186045585 false] [17592186069538 1071 "Find the general solution to the differential equation ${y}^{′}-\\sqrt{y}=2x\\sqrt{y}$ for $y\\ne 0.$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186069538 1074 "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-\\sqrt{y} & =2x\\sqrt{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\sqrt{y} & =2x\\sqrt{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x\\sqrt{y}+\\sqrt{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =(2x+1)\\sqrt{y} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =(2x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int (2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 2\\sqrt{y} & ={x}^{2}+x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n2\\sqrt{y}={x}^{2}+x+C\n$$\n\nWe're required to write the solution in the form $y=y(x).$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={(\\frac{{x}^{2}+x+C}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186069539 1068 #edb/ref 17592186068374 13194139533430 17592186045585 false] [17592186069539 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350786 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186069539 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186069539 1071 "Find the general solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)y=0$ for $y\\ne 0.$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186069539 1071 "Find the general solution to the differential equation $\\frac{dy}{dx} - (\\cos x)y = 0$ for $y \\ne 0$." 13194139533430 17592186045585 false] [17592186069539 1074 "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)y & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)y \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|y| & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C.\n$$\n\nWe're required to write the solution in the form $y=y(x).$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y| & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C} \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot {e}^{C} \\\\ |y| & =K{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K={e}^{C}$ and $K>0.$\n\nWe can drop the absolute value bars by allowing $K$ to be either positive or negative. Therefore, the most general solution is\n\n$$\ny=K{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186078473 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351504 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186078490 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351516 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186083159 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351341 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186083160 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351338 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186083160 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351339 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186083161 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351340 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186083208 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351462 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186083213 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351445 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186083213 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351447 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186083213 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351448 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186083213 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351450 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186083213 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351451 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186083213 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351453 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186083238 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351560 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186083312 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351559 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186083315 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351529 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186083316 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351532 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186083316 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351565 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186083349 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351457 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186083399 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351318 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186083399 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351351 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186083400 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351358 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186083400 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351359 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186083400 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351360 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186083400 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351361 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186083944 1068 #edb/ref 17592186082565 13194139533430 17592186045585 false] [17592186083944 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350849 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186083944 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186083944 1071 "The length of a curve $y = f(x)$ is given by $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{2 + 4x^{2} - 4x}dx$. Which of the following could be an equation for this curve?" 13194139533430 17592186045585 false] [17592186083944 1071 "The length of a curve $y=f(x)$ is given by ${\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{2+4{x}^{2}-4x}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Which of the following could be  an equation for this curve?" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186083944 1074 "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe're given that \n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{2+4{x}^{2}-4x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComparing the above with the arc length formula, we see that we must have\n\n$$\n[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}=4{x}^{2}-4x+1=(2x-1)^{2}\\,⟹\\,{f}^{′}(x)=\\pm (2x-1).\n$$\n\nIntegrating the above equation with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\pm \\int (2x-1)\\text{ d}x \\\\  & =\\pm ({x}^{2}-x)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, the only one that has the required form is $y={x}^{2}-x+4.$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186083945 1068 #edb/ref 17592186082571 13194139533430 17592186045585 false] [17592186083945 1068 #edb/ref 17592186351160 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186083945 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186083945 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to\n$\\displaystyle \\int_{-6}^{-3}\\frac{dx}{\\sqrt{x^{2} - 9}}$?" 13194139533430 17592186045585 false] [17592186083945 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to \n${\\int }_{-6}^{-3}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-9}}?$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186083945 1074 "This is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded as we approach the *upper* integration limit from any point that lies in the interval $x\\in (-6,-3):$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-9}}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to (-3)^{-}\n$$\n\nWe can attempt to evaluate this integral by integrating $f(x)$ over the interval $x\\in [-6,b]$ and taking the limit as $b\\to (-3)^{-}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-6}^{-3}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-9}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{b\\to (-3)^{-}}({\\int }_{-6}^{b}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-9}}\\,\\text{d}x)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186083950 1068 #edb/ref 17592186082601 13194139533430 17592186045585 false] [17592186083950 1068 #edb/ref 17592186351062 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186083950 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186083950 1071 "Several children were asked their age. The age of each child (in years) was recorded below. If $X$ is the age of a randomly selected child, what is the expected value of $X$?\n\n| Age | $10$ | $11$ | $12$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Number of Children | $2$ | $4$ | $4$ |\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533430 17592186045585 false] [17592186083950 1071 "Several children were asked their age. The age of each child (in years) was recorded below. If $X$ is the age of a randomly selected child, what is the expected value of $X?$\n\n| Age | $10$ | $11$ | $12$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Number of Children | $2$ | $4$ | $4$ |" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186083950 1074 "The expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}x\\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nWe're given the frequencies of the children's ages, so we can start by computing the corresponding probabilities. The total number of children is $2+4+4=10,$ so the corresponding probabilities are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=10)=f(10) & =\\frac{2}{10}=\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ P(X=11)=f(11) & =\\frac{4}{10}=\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ P(X=12)=f(12) & =\\frac{4}{10}=\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe organize these results in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $10$ | $11$ | $12$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ | $\\frac{2}{5}$ | $\\frac{2}{5}$ |\n\nNow, summing up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =10\\cdot f(10)+11\\cdot f(11)+12\\cdot f(12) \\\\  & =10(\\frac{1}{5})+11(\\frac{2}{5})+12(\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\  & =2+\\frac{22}{5}+\\frac{24}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{5}+\\frac{22}{5}+\\frac{24}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{10+22+24}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{56}{5} \\\\  & =11.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186087724 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351452 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186087725 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351454 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186087725 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351455 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186087736 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351407 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186087737 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351412 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186087740 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351169 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186087740 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351173 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186087740 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351178 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186087752 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351477 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186087752 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351478 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186087756 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351283 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186087757 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351181 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186087757 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351183 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186087764 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351442 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186087764 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351443 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186087767 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351399 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186087773 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351521 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186087785 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351238 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186087785 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351244 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186087785 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351247 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186087791 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351223 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186087793 1038 #edb/ref 1