tion of $T?$\n\n- $(4,-1)$\n- $(17,-3)$\n- $(10,-1)$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186088394 1074 "To find the image of $S$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 17 & 10 \\\\ -1 & -3 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(4,-1),(17,-3),(10,-1).$ \n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186088395 1068 #edb/ref 17592186086993 13194139533430 17592186045585 false] [17592186088395 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350887 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186088395 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186088395 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-{y}^{′}-12y=10{e}^{2x},\\,y(0)=0,\\text{ }\\text{ }{y}^{′}(0)=2.\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186088395 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n$y^{″} - y^{′} - 12y = 10e^{2x}, y(0) = 0, y^{′}(0) = 2$." 13194139533430 17592186045585 false] [17592186088395 1074 "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-{y}^{′}-12y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -12 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)(\\lambda -4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-3$ or $\\lambda =4$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $10{e}^{2x}.$ In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $2$, which is not the root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha {e}^{2x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=4\\alpha {e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p},$ ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\alpha {e}^{2x}-2\\alpha {e}^{2x}-12(\\alpha {e}^{2x}) & =10{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\alpha (4-2-12){e}^{2x} & =10{e}^{2x} \\\\ -10\\alpha {e}^{2x} & =10{e}^{2x} \\\\ -10\\alpha  & =10 \\\\ \\alpha  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{4x}-{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nLet us find constants $A$ and $B.$ \n\nSubstituting $y(0)=0$ into the general solution gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+B-1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=2.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-3A{e}^{-3x}+4B{e}^{4x}-2{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=2$ into the above gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3A+4B-2 & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations for $A$ and $B$:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}A+B=1 \\\\ -3A+4B=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=0,B=1.$\n\nWe substitute this into the general particular integral and find that the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{4x}-{e}^{2x}.\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186088399 1068 #edb/ref 17592186087017 13194139533430 17592186045585 false] [17592186088399 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350844 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186088399 1070 #edb/ref 175