peratorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(2\\beta -4\\alpha )\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+(-2\\alpha -4\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-4\\alpha +2\\beta =2\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ -4\\beta -2\\alpha =1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{1}{2},$ $\\beta =0.$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{x}-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186088400 1068 #edb/ref 17592186087023 13194139533430 17592186045585 false] [17592186088400 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350770 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186088400 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186088400 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+y=-25\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x,\\,y(0)=4,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0.\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186088400 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n$y^{″} + 2y^{′} + y =-25\\sin 2x, y(0) = 4, y^{′}(0) = 0$." 13194139533430 17592186045585 false] [17592186088400 1074 "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +1 & =0 \\\\ {(\\lambda +1)}^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-1$ is a repeated root, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $-25\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.$ Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x} & =-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =-4\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-4\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(-4\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-4\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)+2(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)+(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)=-25\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(-3\\alpha +4\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+(-4\\alpha -3\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=-25\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-3\\alpha +4\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x) \\\\ -4\\alpha -3\\beta =-25\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =4$ and $\\beta =3.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nNow let us find constants $A$ and $B.$ \n\nSubstituting $y(0)=4$ into the general solution gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+4 & =4 \\\\ A & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{-x}-Bx{e}^{-x}-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ into the above gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-A+B+6 & =0 \\\\ B & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A=0,B=-6.$\n\nSo, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-6x{e}^{-x}+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186088401 1068 #edb/ref 17592186087029 13194139533430 17592186045585 false] [17592186088401 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350757 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186088401 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186088401 1071 "Find the general solution to the  equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+4y=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186088401 1071 "Find the general solution to the equation\n$\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} + 4y = 8\\cos 2x - \\sin 2x$." 13194139533430 17592186045585 false] [17592186088401 1074 "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+4y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =\\pm \\text{2i}$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$ \n\nThe particular solution of the inhomogeneous equation cannot be of the form $\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ because this is already included in the complementary function. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x} & =(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)+2x(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =4(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)-4x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n4(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)-4x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)+4x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}4\\beta =8\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x) \\\\ -4\\alpha =-1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{1}{4}$ and $\\beta =2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=x(\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+x(\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x).\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186088407 1068 #edb/ref 17592186087065 13194139533430 17592186045585 false] [17592186088407 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350977 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186088407 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186088407 1071 "![](images/07/07724ed5ffd4843ab0a23b0029eeec6291ae698aba71f56f99fb7ac565287932.png)\n\nThe Argand diagram above shows the roots of a quartic polynomial $f(x)$. Find $f(x)$ given that the coefficient of the quartic term is $1$." 13194139533430 17592186045585 false] [17592186088407 1071 "![](images/07/07724ed5ffd4843ab0a23b0029eeec6291ae698aba71f56f99fb7ac565287932.png)\n\nThe Argand diagram above shows the roots of a quartic polynomial $f(x).$ Find $f(x)$ given that the coefficient of the quartic term is $1.$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186088407 1074 "From the diagram, we see that $f(x)$ has roots ${x}_{1}=-1,$ ${x}_{2}=4,$ and ${x}_{3,4}=\\pm 2\\text{i}.$ Additionally, we're given that the coefficient of the quartic term is $a=1.$ Therefore, we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2})(x-{x}_{3})(x-{x}_{4}) \\\\  & =1(x-(-1))(x-4)(x-(-2\\text{i}))(x-(2\\text{i})) \\\\  & =1(x+1)(x-4)(x+2\\text{i})(x-2\\text{i})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)(x-4)(x+2\\text{i})(x-2\\text{i}) & = \\\\ ({x}^{2}+x-4x-4)({x}^{2}+(2\\text{i}-2\\text{i})x-(2\\text{i})^{2}) & = \\\\ ({x}^{2}-3x-4)({x}^{2}+4) & = \\\\ {x}^{4}+4{x}^{2}-3{x}^{3}-12x-4{x}^{2}-16 & = \\\\ {x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}-12x-16 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)={x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}-12x-16.$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186088408 1068 #edb/ref 17592186087071 13194139533430 17592186045585 false] [17592186088408 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350763 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186088408 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186088408 1071 "What is the complex conjugate of $z = 3 + 5\\textrm{i}$?" 13194139533430 17592186045585 false] [17592186088408 1071 "What is the complex conjugate of $z=3+5\\text{i}?$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186088408 1074 "To find the complex conjugate, we flip the sign of the imaginary part.\n\nIn the given number $z=3+5\\text{i},$ the imaginary part is $5\\text{i}.$ Flipping the sign, we get $-5\\text{i}.$\n\nTherefore, the complex conjugate is given by $\\overset{―}{z}=3-5\\text{i}.$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186088420 1068 #edb/ref 17592186087143 13194139533430 17592186045585 false] [17592186088420 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350784 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186088420 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186088420 1071 "The outstanding amount $L(t)$ that Daphne needs to repay the bank $t$ years after taking out a loan can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}t}=-0.5L.\n$$\n\nIf she borrowed $$32\\,000,$ calculate the debt remaining after $8$ years. Round your answer to the nearest dollar." 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186088420 1071 "The outstanding amount $L(t)$ that Daphne needs to repay the bank $t$ years after taking out a loan can be modeled by the differential equation\n$\\frac{dL}{dt} =-0.5L$.\n\nIf she borrowed $$32000$, calculate the debt remaining after $8$ years. Round your answer to the nearest dollar.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533430 17592186045585 false] [17592186088420 1074 "We model the amount of debt remaining using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}t}=-0.5L.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nL(t)={L}_{0}\\,{e}^{-0.5t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${L}_{0}$ is the initial value.\n\nWe're told that $$32\\,000$ were borrowed, so we have ${L}_{0}=32\\,000.$\n\nTherefore, the amount of debt $L$ remaining at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nL(t)=32\\,000\\,{e}^{-0.5t}\\,.\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the amount of debt remaining when $t=8,$ we substitute $t=8$ into the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(8) & =32\\,000\\,{e}^{-0.5\\cdot 8} \\\\  & =32\\,000\\,{e}^{-4} \\\\  & ≈586.100\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounding our answer to the nearest integer, we conclude that $586$ dollars of debt remain." 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186088424 1068 #edb/ref 17592186087167 13194139533430 17592186045585 false] [17592186088424 1068 #edb/ref 17592186351120 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186088424 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186088424 1074 "To determine which of the following differential equations is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation, we attempt to write each option in the standard form.\n\nA first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nNote that the option\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2xy=6{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nis already is written in standard form with  $P(x)=2x$ and $Q(x)=6{x}^{2}.$ Since the equation is written in standard form, we conclude that it is a first-order linear ODE.\n\nAll of the other equations cannot be written in the standard form, and therefore they are not first-order linear ODEs." 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186088425 1068 #edb/ref 17592186087173 13194139533430 17592186045585 false] [17592186088425 1068 #edb/ref 17592186351076 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186088425 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186088425 1074 "To determine which of the following differential equations is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation, we attempt to write each option in the standard form.\n\nA first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nLet's attempt to write $y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+x=y$ in the standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+x=y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+x{y}^{-1}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation cannot be written in standard form since the power of $y$ in the term $x{y}^{-1}$ is not $1.$ Since the equation cannot be written in standard form, we conclude that it is not a first-order linear ODE.\n\nAll of the other equations can be written in the standard form, and therefore they are first-order linear ODEs." 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186088426 1068 #edb/ref 17592186087179 13194139533430 17592186045585 false] [17592186088426 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350860 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186088426 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186088426 1071 "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2x-y}{x}\\,.\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186088426 1071 "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{2x - y}{x}$." 13194139533430 17592186045585 false] [17592186088426 1074 "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2x-y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2x}{x}-\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & +\\frac{y}{x}=\\frac{2x}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & +{{\\frac{1}{x}}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}y={{2}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$ and $Q(x)=2.$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186092230 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351378 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186092230 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351379 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186092230 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351380 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186092230 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351381 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186092275 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351463 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186092275 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351464 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186092276 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351466 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186092279 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351273 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186092279 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351274 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186092280 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351382 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186092280 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351384 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186092280 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351385 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186092339 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351426 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186092351 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351395 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186092351 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351396 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186092353 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351439 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186092355 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351440 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186092355 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351441 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186092381 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351305 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186092381 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351306 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186096705 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351488 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186097379 1068 #edb/ref 17592186096168 13194139533430 17592186045585 false] [17592186097379 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350817 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186097379 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186097379 1071 "Given that  ${a}_{n}={n}^{2}-5$ for $n\\ge 1,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${a}_{n+1}-{a}_{n}\\le 0$ for all $n\\ge 1$\n- ${a}_{n}$ is an increasing sequence\n- ${a}_{n}$ is a decreasing sequence" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186097379 1071 "Given that $a_{n} = n^{2} - 5$ for $n \\ge 1$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $a_{n + 1} - a_{n} \\le 0$ for all $n \\ge 1$\n2. $a_{n}$ is an increasing sequence\n3. $a_{n}$ is a decreasing sequence" 13194139533430 17592186045585 false] [17592186097379 1074 "Suppose the sequence ${a}_{n}$ is defined for $n\\ge 1.$ To determine whether ${a}_{n}$ is increasing or decreasing, we can use the following test:\n\n- If a sequence ${a}_{n}$ is *increasing,* then for all $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}\\ge {a}_{n}\\,⟹\\,{a}_{n+1}-{a}_{n}\\ge 0.\n$$\n\n- If a sequence ${a}_{n}$ is *decreasing,* then for all $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}\\le {a}_{n}\\,⟹\\,{a}_{n+1}-{a}_{n}\\le 0.\n$$\n\n- If neither of these conditions holds, the sequence is *neither increasing nor decreasing*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the difference ${a}_{n+1}-{a}_{n},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n+1}-{a}_{n} & =(n+1)^{2}-5-({n}^{2}-5) \\\\  & =(n+1)^{2}-5-{n}^{2}+5 \\\\  & ={n}^{2}+2n+1-{n}^{2} \\\\  & =2n+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is *positive* for $n\\ge 1.$\n\nTo see this concretely, set ${b}_{n}=2n+1.$ Computing the first few terms of ${b}_{n}$ we get\n\n$$\n3,\\,5,\\,7,\\,9,\\,…\\,.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, whereas statement III is false. Since ${a}_{n+1}-{a}_{n}\\ge 0$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ we can conclude that ${a}_{n}$ is an increasing sequence.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186100899 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351486 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186100899 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351487 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186100934 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351402 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186100934 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351456 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186100937 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351349 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186100943 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351214 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186100943 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351215 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186100943 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351542 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186100943 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351543 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186100944 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351536 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186100948 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351563 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186100948 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351564 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186100949 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351561 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186100949 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351562 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186101551 1068 #edb/ref 17592186100437 13194139533430 17592186045585 false] [17592186101551 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350808 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186101551 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186101551 1071 "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x}$ that satisfies $y(1)=2.$ Assume that $x>0.$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186101551 1071 "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{y^{2}}{x}$ that satisfies $y(1) = 2$. Assume that $x > 0$." 13194139533430 17592186045585 false] [17592186101551 1074 "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find the particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -\\frac{1}{y} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{y} & =-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAssuming $x>0,$ the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+C},\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now apply the condition $y(1)=2$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & =2 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+C} & =2 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{C} & =2 \\\\ C & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the particular solution of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x}$ that satisfies the condition $y(1)=2$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{-1}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-\\frac{1}{2})}=\\frac{2}{1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}\\,.\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186105771 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351296 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186105772 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351184 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186105772 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351189 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186105773 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351312 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186105823 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351549 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186105823 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351552 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186105823 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351553 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186105823 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351554 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186105826 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351556 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186105928 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351304 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186106497 1068 #edb/ref 17592186105100 13194139533430 17592186045585 false] [17592186106497 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350773 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186106497 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186106497 1071 "What linear transformation is represented by the following matrix\n$[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 12 & 1\\end{aligned}]?$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186106497 1071 "What linear transformation is represented by the following matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 12 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$?" 13194139533430 17592186045585 false] [17592186106497 1074 "We can find the images of the standard basis vectors $i$ and $j$ under the action of  $T,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(i) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 12 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]=i+12j \\\\ T(j) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 12 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix represents a *vertical* shear with a shear factor of $12.$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186106498 1068 #edb/ref 17592186105106 13194139533430 17592186045585 false] [17592186106498 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350865 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186106498 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186106498 1071 "![](images/47/478ea8a880f959248ae2885776ca3c2b3774935f96ddb64ce531adc6fc5b3ef7.png)\n\nFind the total area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve $y = \\frac{1}{9 + x^{2}}$ and the $x$-axis, as shown above." 13194139533430 17592186045585 false] [17592186106498 1071 "![](images/47/478ea8a880f959248ae2885776ca3c2b3774935f96ddb64ce531adc6fc5b3ef7.png)\n\nFind the total area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve $y=\\frac{1}{9+{x}^{2}}$ and the $x$-axis, as shown above." 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186106498 1074 "We find the area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve and the $x$-axis by calculating the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{9+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{9+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{1}{9+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{9+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the first of these integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{1}{9+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{9+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{3}{9+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\frac{x}{3}|}_{a}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\frac{a}{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\frac{a}{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{9+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{9+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{1}{9+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{9+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186106505 1068 #edb/ref 17592186105148 13194139533430 17592186045585 false] [17592186106505 1068 #edb/ref 17592186351012 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186106505 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186106505 1071 "Which of the following functions are valid probability mass functions?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}, & x=3 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}, & x=7\\end{aligned}\\,g(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}, & x=3 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}, & x=7\\end{aligned}\\,h(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}, & x=3 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}, & x=7\\end{aligned}\\,\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186106505 1071 "Which of the following functions are valid probability mass functions?\n\n$f(x) = {\\frac{1}{2},\\begin{vmatrix}x = 3 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3},\\end{vmatrix}x = 7g(x) = {\\frac{1}{3},\\begin{vmatrix}x = 3 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3},\\end{vmatrix}x = 7h(x) = {\\frac{3}{2},\\begin{vmatrix}x = 3 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2},\\end{vmatrix}x = 7$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 false] [17592186106505 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability mass function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $0\\le f(x)\\le 1$ for all $x$ in $S$\n\n- ${\\sum }_{x\\in S}f(x)=1$\n\nLet's check the above conditions for each of the given functions.\n\n- The function $f$ is *not* a valid probability mass function because it does not satisfy the second condition. The values of $f$ do not sum to $1:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{2}{3}=\\frac{7}{6}\\ne 1\n$$\n\n- The function $g$ is a valid probability mass function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n0\\le \\frac{1}{3},\\frac{2}{3}\\le 1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}=1.\n$$\n\n- The function $h$ is *not* a valid probability distribution because the value $h(3)=\\frac{3}{2}$ does not satisfy the first condition (it is not between $0$ and $1$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$g$ only.\"" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186106506 1068 #edb/ref 17592186105154 13194139533430 17592186045585 false] [17592186106506 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350894 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186106506 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186106506 1071 "| $x$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $P(X=x)$ | $\\frac{1}{9}$ | $\\frac{2}{9}$ | $\\frac{4}{9}$ | $\\frac{2}{9}$ |\n\nCompute $P(X\\in {\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2}})$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability mass function shown in the table above." 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186106506 1071 "| $x$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $P(X = x)$ | $\\frac{1}{9}$ | $\\frac{2}{9}$ | $\\frac{4}{9}$ | $\\frac{2}{9}$ |\n\nCompute $P(X ∈ {\\frac{1}{2}, \\frac{3}{2}})$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability mass function shown in the table above.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533430 17592186045585 false] [17592186106506 1074 "Using the values in the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\in {\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2}}) & =P(X=\\frac{1}{2})+P(X=\\frac{3}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{4}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186115213 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351393 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186115214 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351391 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186115214 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351392 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186115291 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351388 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186115291 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351525 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186115292 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351389 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186119754 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351533 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186119754 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351534 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186119755 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351535 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186119756 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351530 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186119829 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351272 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186119830 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351166 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186119830 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351168 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186119830 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351275 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186119831 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351170 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186119831 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351171 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186119831 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351175 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186119831 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351177 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186119831 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351276 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186119953 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351512 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186119953 1038 #edb/ref 175