7592186128743 13194139533430 17592186045585 false] [17592186129882 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350794 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186129882 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186129882 1071 "What is the range of $f(x) = \\frac{1}{x - 6} + 1$?" 13194139533430 17592186045585 false] [17592186129882 1071 "What is the range of $f(x)=\\frac{1}{x-6}+1?$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186129882 1074 "A transformed reciprocal function of the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{a}{bx+c}+d\n$$\n\nhas the horizontal asymptote $y=d,$ and therefore, its range is\n\n$$\nf(x)\\in (-\\infty ,d)\\cup (d,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $d=1.$ Therefore, the horizontal asymptote is $y=1,$ and the range of the function is\n\n$$\nf(x)\\in (-\\infty ,1)\\cup (1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe graph $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/f6/f6b1316dd9c7f2a40c23de08dde63cbc6402593c6209d99384279a7c1ae29da6.png)" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186129885 1068 #edb/ref 17592186128761 13194139533430 17592186045585 false] [17592186129885 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350870 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186129885 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186129885 1071 "Given that the discrete random variable $Y$ has the probability mass function $f(y)$ and the cumulative distribution function $F(y)$ as shown in the table below, compute $a + b + c$.\n\n| $y$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(y)$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.2$ | $0.4$ | $0.1$ |\n| $F(y)$ | $0.1$ | $a$ | $0.5$ | $b$ | $c$ |\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533430 17592186045585 false] [17592186129885 1071 "Given that the discrete random variable $Y$ has the probability mass function $f(y)$ and the cumulative distribution function $F(y)$ as shown in the table below, compute $a+b+c.$\n\n| $y$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(y)$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.2$ | $0.4$ | $0.1$ |\n| $F(y)$ | $0.1$ | $a$ | $0.5$ | $b$ | $c$ |" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186129885 1074 "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $Y$ is a function $F(y)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(y)=P(Y\\le y).\n$$\n\nComputing $a,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =F(1) \\\\  & =P(Y\\le 1) \\\\  & =P(Y\\in {0,1}) \\\\  & =f(0)+f(1) \\\\  & =0.1+0.2 \\\\  & =0.3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $b,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =F(3) \\\\  & =P(Y\\le 3) \\\\  & =P(Y\\in {0,1,2,3}) \\\\  & =f(0)+f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & =0.1+0.2+0.2+0.4 \\\\  & =0.9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $c,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =F(4) \\\\  & =P(Y\\le 4) \\\\  & =P(Y\\in {0,1,2,3,4}) \\\\  & =f(0)+f(1)+f(2)+f(3)+f(4) \\\\  & =0.1+0.2+0.2+0.4+0.1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\na+b+c=0.3+0.9+1=2.2.\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186129889 1068 #edb/ref 17592186128785 13194139533430 17592186045585 false] [17592186129889 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350851 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186129889 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186129889 1071 "Given that the discrete random variable $X$ has the cumulative distribution function $F(x)$ shown in the table below, compute $P(4 < X \\le 6)$.\n\n| $x$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ | $7$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $F(x)$ | $0.3$ | $0.4$ | $0.6$ | $0.9$ | $1.0$ |" 13194139533430 17592186045585 false] [17592186129889 1071 "Given that the discrete random variable $X$ has the cumulative distribution function $F(x)$ shown in the table below, compute $P(4<X\\le 6).$\n\n| $x$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ | $7$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $F(x)$ | $0.3$ | $0.4$ | $0.6$ | $0.9$ | $1.0$ |" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186129889 1074 "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $X$ is a function $F(x)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x).\n$$\n\nTo compute the probability of a random variable $X$ over an interval, we can use the rule\n\n$$\nP(a<X\\le b)=F(b)-F(a).\n$$\n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(4<X\\le 6) & =F(6)-F(4) \\\\  & =0.9-0.4 \\\\  & =0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186129890 1068 #edb/ref 17592186128791 13194139533430 17592186045585 false] [17592186129890 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350814 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186129890 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186129890 1071 "Given that the discrete random variable $Y$ has the cumulative distribution function $F(y)$ shown in the table below, compute $P(\\frac{1}{2} \\le Y \\le \\frac{3}{2})$.\n\n| $y$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $F(y)$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.4$ | $0.8$ | $1.0$ |" 13194139533430 17592186045585 false] [17592186129890 1071 "Given that the discrete random variable $Y$ has the cumulative distribution function $F(y)$ shown in the table below, compute $P(\\frac{1}{2}\\le Y\\le \\frac{3}{2}).$\n\n| $y$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $F(y)$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.4$ | $0.8$ | $1.0$ |" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186129890 1074 "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $Y$ is a function $F(y)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(y)=P(Y\\le y).\n$$\n\nTo compute the probability of a random variable $Y$ over an interval, we can use the rule\n\n$$\nP(a<Y\\le b)=F(b)-F(a).\n$$\n\nHere, we want to compute $P(\\frac{1}{2}\\le Y\\le \\frac{3}{2}).$ However, this interval is stated in the form $a\\le Y\\le b,$ not $a<Y\\le b.$\n\nSo first, we must convert the interval $\\frac{1}{2}\\le Y\\le \\frac{3}{2}$ to the form $a<Y\\le b.$ Looking at the given possible values of $Y,$ we see that $\\frac{1}{2}\\le Y$ is equivalent to $0<Y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\frac{1}{2}\\le Y\\le \\frac{3}{2}) & =P(0<Y\\le \\fra