 the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =j+t(3\\,i+3\\,j-j) \\\\  & =j+t(3\\,i+2\\,j) \\\\  & =3t\\,i+(1+2t)\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x(t)=3t$ and $y(t)=1+2t$ along the segment.\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=3\\,i+2\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}\\,dx-y\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t-y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(3t)^{2}\\cdot 3-(1+2t)\\cdot 2\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}27{t}^{2}-2-4t\\,dt \\\\  & ={[9{t}^{3}-2t-2{t}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[9(1)^{3}-2(1)-2(1)^{2}]-[0] \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186180292 1068 #edb/ref 17592186179266 13194139533430 17592186045585 false] [17592186180292 1068 #edb/ref 17592186351096 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186180292 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186180292 1071 "If the path $C$ is a part of the parabola $y = (x + 1)^{2}$ traversed from the point $P(0, 1)$ to point $Q(1, 4)$, then $\\displaystyle \\int_(C)\\frac{dx + dy}{y} =$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 false] [17592186180292 1071 "If the path $C$ is a part of the parabola $y=(x+1)^{2}$ traversed from the point $P(0,1)$ to point $Q(1,4),$ then ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{dx+dy}{y}=$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186180292 1074 "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y=(x+1)^{2}.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=t\\,i+(t+1)^{2}\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,1]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=i+2(t+1)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{dx+dy}{y} & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{y}(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{y}(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1+2(t+1)}{(t+1)^{2}}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{(t+1)^{2}}+\\frac{2}{t+1}\\,dt \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{t+1}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[-\\frac{1}{1+1}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+1)]-[-\\frac{1}{0+1}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0+1)] \\\\  & =[-\\frac{1}{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2]-[-1+0] \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186180293 1068 #edb/ref 17592186179272 13194139533430 17592186045585 false] [17592186180293 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350779 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186180293 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186180293 1071 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_(C)\\frac{ydy - xdx}{x^{2} + y^{2}}$, where $C$ is part of the circle $x^{2} + y^{2} = 1$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $(1, 0)$ to the point $(0, 1)$." 13194139533430 17592186045585 false] [17592186180293 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{y\\,dy-x\\,dx}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},$ where $C$ is part of the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $(1,0)$ to the point $(0,1).$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186180293 1074 "Since $C$ is a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin, it can be parametrized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{y\\,dy-x\\,dx}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{y\\cdot {y}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t-x\\cdot {x}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{y\\cdot {y}^{′}-x\\cdot {x}^{′}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}{{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}[-1-1] \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186180295 1068 #edb/ref 17592186179284 13194139533430 17592186045585 false] [17592186180295 1068 #edb/ref 17592186351108 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186180295 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186180295 1071 "The growth rate of the squirrel population $P(t)$ in a certain forest is jointly proportional to the squirrel population at time $t$, measured in years, and the difference between the forest's carrying capacity and the squirrel population. The constant of proportionality is $0.00001$ and the carrying capacity is $5000$. How many squirrels will there be in the forest after $10$ years given that there were $4000$ squirrels initially? Round your answer to the nearest integer.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533430 17592186045585 false] [17592186180295 1071 "The growth rate of the squirrel population $P(t)$ in a certain forest is jointly proportional to the squirrel population at time $t,$ measured in years, and the difference between the forest's carrying capacity and the squirrel population. The constant of proportionality is $0.000\\,01$ and the carrying capacity is $5000.$ How many squirrels will there be in the forest after $10$ years given that there were $4000$ squirrels initially? Round your answer to the nearest integer." 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186180295 1074 "Since the squirrel population's growth rate is *jointly* proportional to the squirrel population at time $t$ and the difference between the carrying capacity and the squirrel population, the squirrel population follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.000\\,01,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=5000.$ Therefore, the squirrel population growth is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.000\\,01P(5000-P).\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{5000}{1+C{e}^{-(5000\\cdot 0.000\\,01)t}}=\\frac{5000}{1+C{e}^{-0.05t}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=4000$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =4000 \\\\ \\frac{5000}{1+C{e}^{-0.05(0)}} & =4000 \\\\ 1+C & =\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ C & =0.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{5000}{1+0.25{e}^{-0.05\\cdot t}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $t=10$ into the expression for $P(t),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(10) & =\\frac{5000}{1+0.25{e}^{-0.05\\cdot 10}} \\\\  & ≈4342.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [1759218618