$\\ln x$ about $x = 1$." 13194139533430 17592186045585 false] [17592186184857 1071 "Find the fourth-degree Taylor polynomial of $\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ about $x=1.$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186184857 1074 "The fourth-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=a$ is given by\n\n$$\n{P}_{4}(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(a)}{3!}(x-a)^{3}+\\frac{{f}^{(4)}(a)}{4!}(x-a)^{4}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $a=1,$ so\n\n$$\n{P}_{4}(x)=f(1)+{f}^{′}(1)(x-1)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(1)}{2!}{(x-1)}^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(1)}{3!}{(x-1)}^{3}+\\frac{{f}^{(4)}(1)}{4!}{(x-1)}^{4}.\n$$\n\nSo we need to work out $f(1),$ ${f}^{′}(1),$ ${f}^{″}(1),$ ${f}^{‴}(1)$ and ${f}^{(4)}(1),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & ⟹ &  & f(1) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡1=0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{x}={x}^{-1} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{′}(1) & ={1}^{-1}=1 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-{x}^{-2} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{″}(1) & =-(1)^{-2}=-1 \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =-(-2){x}^{-3}=2{x}^{-3} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{‴}(1) & =2(1)^{-3}=2 \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(x) & =2(-3){x}^{-4}=-6{x}^{-4} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{(4)}(1) & =-6(1)^{-4}=-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${P}_{4}(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{4}(x) & =0+(x-1)-\\frac{1}{2!}(x-1)^{2}+\\frac{2}{3!}(x-1)^{3}-\\frac{6}{4!}(x-1)^{4} \\\\  & =(x-1)-\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}(x-1)^{3}-\\frac{1}{4}(x-1)^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186184858 1068 #edb/ref 17592186183727 13194139533430 17592186045585 false] [17592186184858 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350823 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186184858 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186184858 1071 "Find the distance between the vector $x$ and the subspace spanned by the linearly independent vectors ${a_{1}, a_{2}}$, where\n\n$a_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, a_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, x = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139533430 17592186045585 false] [17592186184858 1071 "Find the distance between the vector $x$ and the subspace spanned by the linearly independent vectors ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186184858 1074 "The orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=\\overset{^}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(x,\\overset{^}{x}) & =‖x-\\overset{^}{x}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{2})^{2}+({x}_{3}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(3-2)^{2}+(-3-(-1))^{2}+(0-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+4+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186198366 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351287 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186198366 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351288 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186198376 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351298 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186198376 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351303 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186198399 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351465 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186199007 1068 #edb/ref 17592186197882 13194139533430 17592186045585 false] [17592186199007 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350857 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186199007 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186199007 1071 "Find the locations of all of the critical points of $T$, where $T$ is defined as\n\n$T: \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}uv \\\\ u + v\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139533430 17592186045585 false] [17592186199007 1071 "Find the locations of all of the critical points of $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}uv \\\\ u+v\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186199007 1074 "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $T,$ we need to solve \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=uv$ and $y(u,v)=u+v.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =u \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}v & u \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =v\\cdot 1-u\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =v-u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ v-u & =0 \\\\ v & =u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $v=u$ defines a line in the $uv$-plane." 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186199008 1068 #edb/ref 17592186197888 13194139533430 17592186045585 false] [17592186199008 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350885 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186199008 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186199008 1071 "Consider all non-critical points of the transformation $T: (u, v) → (x(u, v), y(u, v))$. The inverse transformation $T^{-1}(x, y) → (u(x, y), v(x, y))$ is given by\n\n$T^{-1}: \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}2xy \\\\ \\frac{y}{x}\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T$ in terms of $u$ and $v$." 13194139533430 17592186045585 false] [17592186199008 1071 "Consider all non-critical points of the transformation $T:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y)\\to (u(x,y),v(x,y))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}2xy \\\\ \\frac{y}{x}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T$ in terms of $u$ and $v.$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186199008 1074 "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $u(x,y)=2xy$ and $v(x,y)=\\frac{y}{x}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =2y & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =2x \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2y & 2x \\\\ -\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2y\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}-2x\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4y}{x} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{y}{x} \\\\  & =4v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4v}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186199009 1068 #edb/ref 17592186197894 13194139533430 17592186045585 false] [17592186199009 1068 #edb/ref 17592186351100 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186199009 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186199009 1071 "Consider the transformation $T: (u, v) → (x(u, v), y(u, v))$. The inverse transformation $T^{-1}(x, y) → (u(x, y), v(x, y))$ is given by\n\n$T^{-1}: \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}2x + 3y \\\\ 2x + y\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T$." 13194139533430 17592186045585 false] [17592186199009 1071 "Consider the transformation $T:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y)\\to (u(x,y),v(x,y))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}2x+3y \\\\ 2x+y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T.$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186199009 1074 "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $u(x,y)=2x+3y$ and $v(x,y)=2x+y.$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =2 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =3 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =2 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1-3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186203967 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351325 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186203968 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351326 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [1759218620396