4139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351162 1072 #uuid "c97b5b71-71aa-5c9f-b66b-7c0744f33544" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351162 1074 "To find every subset of $P,$ we list out every combination of elements within $P.$ \n\nWe can use the following diagram to visualize the process:\n\n![](images/17/17d796ca9f78e72d15b6f29565d03a5ea42c1e2fe544acd02bc3f368993cdb9c.png)\n\nNote that we must include the empty set, since it is a subset of every set.\n\nBy doing this, we get\n\n$$\n∅,{10},{20},{10,20}.\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351163 1068 #edb/ref 17592186351093 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351163 1069 "q-80808" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351163 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351163 1071 "Find all of the subsets of $Q={\\alpha ,\\beta ,\\gamma }.$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351163 1072 #uuid "dd5b17e8-78b5-59d5-b563-f6d50839e764" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351163 1074 "To find every subset of $Q,$ we list out every combination of elements within $Q.$\n\nWe can use the following diagram to visualize the process: \n\n![](images/56/56d0ff0c0e539286b6c15496c7ad295008779f07f4e9132280c787b6303902c0.png)\n\nNote that we must include the empty set since it is a subset of every set.\n\nBy doing this, we get\n\n$$\n∅,{\\alpha },{\\beta },{\\gamma },{\\alpha ,\\beta },{\\alpha ,\\gamma },{\\beta ,\\gamma },{\\alpha ,\\beta ,\\gamma }.\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351164 1068 #edb/ref 17592186351134 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351164 1069 "q-80809" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351164 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351164 1071 "Find all of the subsets of $U={-\\pi ,\\pi }.$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351164 1072 #uuid "5ade80e9-fb10-5f67-9c65-ab5d8d98adbc" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351164 1074 "To find every subset of $U,$ we list out every combination of elements within $U.$ \n\nWe can use the following diagram to visualize the process:\n\n![](images/b0/b0e483a2d10cba17fb2bd3bf0e78d7b4d2fc07b22dbb5c492564b998bbfce6fc.png)\n\nNote that we must include the empty set since it is a subset of every set.\n\nBy doing this, we get\n\n$$\n∅,{-\\pi },{\\pi },{-\\pi ,\\pi }.\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351165 1068 #edb/ref 17592186351144 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351165 1069 "q-80812" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351165 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351165 1071 "Which of the following are subsets of ${Q}^{+}$  (the set of positive rational numbers)?\n\n- ${Q}_{0}^{+}$ (the set of non-negative rational numbers)\n- $N$\n- $Z$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351165 1072 #uuid "94ae6b8c-148f-5682-9bab-a030784afd5c" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351165 1074 "$A$ is a subset of $B$ if every element of $A$ is an element of $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect the given choices.\n\n- ${Q}_{0}^{+}$ is not a subset of ${Q}^{+}.$ For example, $0\\in {Q}_{0}^{+}$ but $0\\notin {Q}^{+}.$\n\n- $N$ is a subset of ${Q}^{+}.$ Indeed, every natural number is also a positive rational number.\n\n- $Z$ is not a subset of ${Q}^{+}.$ For example, $-1\\in Z$ but $-1\\notin {Q}^{+}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351166 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350791 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351166 1069 "q-80814" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351166 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351166 1071 "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ be a basis of ${R}^{3}$ and $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}},$ where ${c}_{1}=-{b}_{2}$, ${c}_{2}=2{b}_{3}$ and ${c}_{3}={b}_{1}?$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351166 1072 #uuid "5391d763-accb-53ad-9d75-896f6a1f5e41" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351166 1074 "Since $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]$, we can write $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={b}_{1}-3{b}_{2}+6{b}_{3} \\\\  & =-3{b}_{2}+6{b}_{3}+{b}_{1} \\\\  & =3(-{b}_{2})+3(2{b}_{3})+{b}_{1} \\\\  & =3{c}_{1}+3{c}_{2}+{c}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351167 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350713 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351167 1069 "q-80818" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351167 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351167 1071 "Let $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}3x+y \\\\ x+2y \\\\ -2x+7y\\end{aligned}]$ be a linear transformation. Determine the value of $k$ for which the vector $v=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 8 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to $\\text{Im}(T).$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351167 1072 #uuid "3ed740eb-f508-5f05-8f3f-7a98f936b4b3" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351167 1074 "The vector $v$ belongs to $\\text{Im}(T)$ if there exists a vector $u=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]$ such that $T(u)=v.$ \n\nTherefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}3x+y \\\\ x+2y \\\\ -2x+7y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 8 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3x+y=9 \\\\ x+2y=8 \\\\ -2x+7y=k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x=8-2y$, which we substitute into the first equation to get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+y & =9 \\\\ 3(8-2y)+y & =9 \\\\ -5y & =-15 \\\\ y & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =8-2y \\\\  & =8-2(3) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=2$ and $y=3$ into the third equation and obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+7y & =k \\\\ -2(2)+7(3) & =k \\\\ k & =17.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $v\\in \\text{Im}(T)$ when $k=17.$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351168 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350795 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351168 1069 "q-80819" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351168 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351168 1071 "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ be a basis of ${R}^{3}$ and $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}].$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}},$ where ${c}_{1}={b}_{1}$, ${c}_{2}={b}_{3}$ and ${c}_{3}=-{b}_{2}?$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351168 1072 #uuid "3376b2e7-02f4-5424-878c-269470eea0cd" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351168 1074 "Since $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]$, we can write $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4{b}_{1}-4{b}_{2}-7{b}_{3} \\\\  & =4{b}_{1}-7{b}_{3}-4{b}_{2} \\\\  & =4({b}_{1})-7({b}_{3})+4(-{b}_{2}) \\\\  & =4{c}_{1}-7{c}_{2}+4{c}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351169 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350742 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351169 1069 "q-8082" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351169 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351169 1071 "![](images/18/18dd1668d0e9eaeb1be7919821707bbf1d472b22b00acf68a921a3522b2f5f7f.png)\n\nWrite the complex number $z$, shown on the Argand diagram above, in the form $r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ giving $\\theta $ in radians to three decimal places." 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351169 1072 #uuid "92d2189e-bd4e-5b46-a533-1fb76600704b" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351169 1074 "The complex number shown in the Argand diagram can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =x+\\text{i}y \\\\  & =-4-2\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo write it in the form $z=r{\\text{e}}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ where $r=|z|$ and $\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z),$ we first calculate $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{20} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $z$ lies in the third quadrant, we can find the angle $\\theta $ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =\\pi +|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})| \\\\  & =\\pi +|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{-2}{-4})| \\\\  & =\\pi +|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})| \\\\  & ≈\\pi +0.463\\,64… \\\\  & ≈3.605.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $r=2\\sqrt{5}$ and $\\theta =3.605$ into $z=r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ we get \n\n$$\nz=2\\sqrt{5}\\,{\\text{e}}^{3.605\\,\\text{i}}\\,.\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351170 1068 #edb/ref 17592186351064 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351170 1069 "q-80826" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351170 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351170 1071 "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}].$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}},$ where ${c}_{1}=4{b}_{1}+{b}_{2}$ and ${c}_{2}=2{b}_{1}?$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351170 1072 #uuid "2c9bca47-36fc-5691-b4a4-81a309a1be29" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351170 1074 "We are given that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}=4{b}_{1}+{b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2}=2{b}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the $b$'s in terms of $c$'s. From the second equation, we get \n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=4(\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{2})+{b}_{2}\\,⟹\\,{b}_{2}={c}_{1}-2{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSince $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]$, we can write the vector $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={b}_{1}-7{b}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{2}-7({c}_{1}-2{c}_{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{2}-7{c}_{1}+14{c}_{2} \\\\  & =-7{c}_{1}+\\frac{29}{2}{c}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ \\frac{29}{2}\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351171 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350802 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351171 1069 "q-80828" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351171 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351171 1071 "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}},$ where ${c}_{1}=3{b}_{2}$ and ${c}_{2}={b}_{1}+6{b}_{2}?$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351171 1072 #uuid "2eedfab1-a2e1-5bd8-81d2-7c293aaffcd9" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351171 1074 "We are given that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}=3{b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2}={b}_{1}+6{b}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the $b$'s in terms of $c$'s. From the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n{b}_{2}=\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{1}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{c}_{2}={b}_{1}+6(\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{1})\\,⟹\\,{b}_{1}={c}_{2}-2{c}_{1}.\n$$\n\nSince $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$, we can write the vector $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-5{b}_{1}+2{b}_{2} \\\\  & =-5({c}_{2}-2{c}_{1})+2(\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{1}) \\\\  & =-5{c}_{2}+10{c}_{1}+\\frac{2}{3}{c}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{3}{c}_{1}-5{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{32}{3} \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351172 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350957 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351172 1069 "q-80829" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351172 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351172 1071 "Which of the following are subsets of ${R}^{-}$ (the set of negative real numbers)?\n\n- ${Z}_{0}^{-}$ (the set of non-positive integers)\n- $N$\n- ${Q}^{-}$  (the set of negative rational numbers)" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351172 1072 #uuid "f854e867-6b9f-52a2-9a74-9314a61b1db2" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351172 1074 "$A$ is a subset of $B$ if every element of $A$ is an element of $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect the given choices.\n\n- ${Z}_{0}^{-}$ is *not* a subset of ${R}^{-}.$ For example, $0\\in {Z}_{0}^{-}$ but $0\\notin {R}^{-}.$\n\n- $N$ is not  a subset of ${R}^{-}.$ For example, $1\\in N$ but $1\\notin {R}^{-}.$\n\n- ${Q}^{-}$ is a subset of ${R}^{-}.$ Indeed, every negative rational number is also a negative real number.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351173 1068 #edb/ref 17592186351028 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351173 1069 "q-8083" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351173 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351173 1071 "![](images/83/83c490278bec718da1338f8926e9d67d2f72909487b9a85bbd386cbf72def2bb.png)\n\nWrite the complex number $z$, shown on the Argand diagram above, in the form $r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ giving $\\theta $ in radians to $3$ decimal places." 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351173 1072 #uuid "e23c3a32-b3bb-5338-8832-25e8c14f3ac5" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351173 1074 "The complex number shown in the Argand diagram can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =x+\\text{i}y \\\\  & =-4+\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo write it in the form $z=r{\\text{e}}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ where $r=|z|$ and $\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z),$ we first calculate $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{17}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $z$ lies in the second quadrant of the complex plane, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})| \\\\  & =\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\frac{1}{4})| \\\\  & ≈2.897.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $r=\\sqrt{17}$ and $\\theta =2.897$ into $z=r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ we get\n\n$$\nz=\\sqrt{17}\\,{\\text{e}}^{2.897\\,\\text{i}}.\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351174 1068 #edb/ref 17592186351084 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351174 1069 "q-80832" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351174 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351174 1071 "Which of the following statements are true for the sets $A={x\\in N:1\\le x<6}$ and $B={x\\in N:3x<9}?$\n\n- $A⊆B$\n- $B⊆A$\n- $A⊈B$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351174 1072 #uuid "1f0cc03e-26f5-51bc-b8ad-bdb1b8cf7d61" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351174 1074 "First, we must find the elements of the sets $A$ and $B.$\n\n- The elements of the set $A$ are all natural numbers greater than or equal to $1$ but less than $6.$ So,\n\n$$\nA={1,2,3,4,5}.\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, the elements of the set $B$ are natural numbers which satisfy the inequality $3x<9.$ So,\n\n$$\nB={1,2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's inspect the given statements.\n\n- $A⊆B$ is false, while $A⊈B$ is true. For example, $5\\in A$ but $5\\notin B.$\n\n- $B⊆A$ is true. Every element in the set $B$ is an element of the set $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351175 1068 #edb/ref 17592186351153 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351175 1069 "q-80835" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351175 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351175 1071 "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}},$ where ${c}_{1}={b}_{1}+{b}_{2}$ and ${c}_{2}={b}_{1}+2{b}_{2}?$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351175 1072 #uuid "cff1c359-b329-5cda-a418-6d006dce3908" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351175 1074 "We are given that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}={b}_{1}+{b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2}={b}_{1}+2{b}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the $b$'s in terms of $c$'s. Multiplying the second equation by $-1$ and adding the equations, we get \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}=-{b}_{2}\\,⟹\\,{b}_{2}={c}_{2}-{c}_{1}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}={b}_{1}+({c}_{2}-{c}_{1})\\,⟹\\,{b}_{1}=2{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSince $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$, we can write the vector $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2{b}_{1}+{b}_{2} \\\\  & =2(2{c}_{1}-{c}_{2})+({c}_{2}-{c}_{1}) \\\\  & =4{c}_{1}-2{c}_{2}+{c}_{2}-{c}_{1} \\\\  & =3{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351176 1068 #edb/ref 17592186351133 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351176 1069 "q-80837" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351176 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351176 1071 "Which of the following statements are true for the sets $A={x\\in {Z}^{+}∣x\\le 3}$ and $B={x\\in Z∣0\\le x<3}?$\n\n- $A⊆B$\n- $B⊈A$\n- $∅⊈B$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351176 1072 #uuid "c643dbca-796e-5e8a-ae4e-2697e41e25d4" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351176 1074 "First, we must find the elements of the sets $A$ and $B.$\n\n- The elements of the set $A$ are all positive integers that satisfy the inequality $x\\le 3.$ So, we have \n\n$$\nA={1,2,3}.\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, the elements of the set $B$ are all integers that satisfy the inequality $0\\le x<3.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nB={0,1,2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's inspect the given statements.\n\n- $A⊆B$ is false. For example, $3\\in A$ but $3\\notin B.$\n\n- $B⊈A$ is true. For example, $0\\in B$ but $0\\notin A.$\n\n- $∅⊈B$ is false. The null set $∅={}$ is a subset of *every* set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351177 1068 #edb/ref 17592186351046 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351177 1069 "q-80838" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351177 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351177 1071 "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}},$ where ${c}_{1}=2{b}_{1}+{b}_{2}$ and ${c}_{2}={b}_{1}-{b}_{2}?$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351177 1072 #uuid "816b4547-6f87-5ca0-b88c-267f913a2f89" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351177 1074 "We are given that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}=2{b}_{1}+{b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2}={b}_{1}-{b}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the $b$'s in terms of $c$'s. Adding the equations, we get \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=3{b}_{1}\\,⟹\\,{b}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{1}+\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=2(\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{1}+\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{2})+{b}_{2}\\,⟹\\,{b}_{2}=\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{1}-\\frac{2}{3}{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nSince $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$, we can write the vector $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =6{b}_{1}-{b}_{2} \\\\  & =6(\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{1}+\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{2})-(\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{1}-\\frac{2}{3}{c}_{2}) \\\\  & =2{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}-\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{1}+\\frac{2}{3}{c}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}{c}_{1}+\\frac{8}{3}{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ \\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351178 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350778 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351178 1069 "q-8084" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351178 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351178 1071 "![](images/a4/a4066e842d7b94527aedd124c113ac9fd0b0f444d0d46152c5794c6f4d022074.png)\n\nWrite the complex number $z$, shown on the Argand diagram above, in the form $r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ giving $\\theta $ in radians to $3$ decimal places." 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351178 1072 #uuid "4f343324-3027-59f8-bdd6-499490a7a521" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351178 1074 "The complex number shown in the Argand diagram can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =x+\\text{i}y \\\\  & =-2+3\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo write it in the form $z=r{\\text{e}}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ where $r=|z|$ and $\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z),$ we first calculate $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{13}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $z$ lies in the second quadrant of the complex plane, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})| \\\\  & =\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\frac{3}{2})| \\\\  & ≈2.159.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $r=\\sqrt{13}$ and $\\theta =2.159$ into $z=r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ we get\n\n$$\nz=\\sqrt{13}\\,{\\text{e}}^{2.159\\,\\text{i}}.\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351179 1068 #edb/ref 17592186351094 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351179 1069 "q-80845" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351179 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351179 1071 "Which of the following statements are true for the sets $A={n\\in Z∣n+1\\ge -1}$ and $B={n\\in Z∣n-2\\ge -4}?$\n\n- $A⊆B$\n- $B⊆A$\n- $B⊈A$\n- $B⊇A$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351179 1072 #uuid "13666e1e-c022-5b9a-b071-b81546a1b503" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351179 1074 "First, we must find the elements of the sets $A$ and $B.$\n\n- The elements of the set $A$ are all the integer numbers that satisfy the inequality $n+1\\ge -1,$ that is to say, $n\\ge -2.$ So, we have \n\n$$\nA={-2,-1,0,1,2,3,4,5,6,…}.\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, the elements of the set $B$ are all integers that satisfy the inequality $n-2\\ge -4,$ That is to say, $n\\ge -2.$ So, we have \n\n$$\nB={-2,-1,0,1,2,3,4,5,6,…}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's inspect the given statements.\n\n- $A⊆B$ is true. Every element in the set $A$ is an element of the set $B.$\n\n- $B⊆A$ is true, while $B⊈A$ is false. Every element in the set $B$ is an element of the set $A.$\n\n- $B⊇A$ is equivalent to $A⊆B,$ which is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and IV only\"." 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351180 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350924 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351180 1069 "q-80869" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351180 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351180 1071 "If $C={1,2,3,4,8,12,24}$ and $D={0,4,8,12,16,20,24},$ then $D∖C=$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351180 1072 #uuid "b5acc2f5-df7c-5705-a2b4-d1d8cb7a7762" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351180 1074 "The set $D∖C$ is the set of all elements belonging to $D$ but *not* $C.$ Note that the elements $4,8,12,24\\in C$ are also in $D,$ so we must remove them. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D∖C & =D-C \\\\  & ={0,4,8,12,16,20,24}-{1,2,3,4,8,12,24} \\\\  & ={0,16,20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351181 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350963 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351181 1069 "q-8087" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351181 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351181 1071 "What linear transformation $T$ is represented by the matrix $T=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{aligned}]?$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351181 1072 #uuid "b2baa132-ec7f-5564-9abe-6e3a16a9d9e2" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351181 1074 "Recall that a dilation of scale factor $k$ is represented by the matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}k & 0 \\\\ 0 & k\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nHere, we have $k=7.$ Therefore, the matrix represents a dilation with a scale factor of $7.$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351182 1068 #edb/ref 17592186351010 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351182 1069 "q-80876" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351182 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351182 1071 "Let $C$  be the set of all multiples of $10$ greater than $0$ and let $D$  be the the set of all multiples of $5$ greater than $0.$ What is $C∖D?$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351182 1072 #uuid "1076728a-78e3-5cde-ae2f-0952fd39a682" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351182 1074 "The multiples of $10$ that are greater than $0$ are\n\n$$\nC={10,20,30,40,50,60,70,…}.\n$$\n\nLikewise, multiples of $5$ that are greater than $0$ are\n\n$$\nD={5,10,15,20,25,30,35,40,45,50,55,60,65,70,75,…}.\n$$\n\nNow, $C∖D$ is the *difference* of $C$ and $D,$ which means that we want to find the elements that are in $C$ but *not* in $D.$\n\nThe elements of $C$ that are also in $D$ are $10,20,30,40,$ and so on (i.e. multiples of $10$). So, we must remove these elements from $C.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C∖D & =C-D \\\\  & ={10,20,30,40,50,60,70,…}-{5,10,15,20,25,30,35,40,45,50,55,60,65,70,75,…} \\\\  & =∅.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351183 1068 #edb/ref 17592186351018 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351183 1069 "q-8088" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351183 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351183 1071 "What linear transformation $T$ is represented by the matrix $T=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}]?$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351183 1072 #uuid "9dd7355b-a34a-52e9-b179-7ba62840a060" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351183 1074 "Recall that a dilation of scale factor $k$ is represented by the matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}k & 0 \\\\ 0 & k\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nHere, we have $k=\\frac{1}{4}.$ Therefore, the matrix represents a dilation with a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{4}.$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351184 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350762 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351184 1069 "q-8089" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351184 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351184 1071 "What linear transformation $T$ is represented by the following matrix\n$[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]?$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351184 1072 #uuid "486355c1-aaab-53b3-8f31-02fd1871e35f" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351184 1074 "We can find the images of the standard basis vectors $i$ and $j$ under the action of  $T,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(i) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=2i \\\\ T(j) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix represents a stretch of scale factor $2$ in the $x$-direction." 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351185 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350862 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351185 1069 "q-80893" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351185 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351185 1071 "What is the cardinality of $X\\times Z$ for $X={11,12,13,...,20}$ and $Z={1,6,8,16,24,30}?$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351185 1072 #uuid "9fc6580f-8efc-5354-b244-c5ea25d4a242" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351185 1074 "For any two finite sets $X$ and $Z,$ the cardinality of $X\\times Z$ is\n\n$$\n|X\\times Z|=|X|\\cdot |Z|.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|X\\times Z| & =|X|\\cdot |Z| \\\\  & =10\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =60.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351186 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350861 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351186 1069 "q-80894" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351186 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351186 1071 "What is the cardinality of $H\\times K$ for $H={1,2,3,…,9}$ and $K={x,y}?$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351186 1072 #uuid "5aedcbc3-5480-58bd-92fe-aaa7bf7e52fa" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351186 1074 "For any two finite sets $H$ and $K,$ the cardinality of $H\\times K$ is\n\n$$\n|H\\times K|=|H|\\cdot |K|.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|H\\times K| & =|H|\\cdot |K| \\\\  & =9\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351187 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350718 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351187 1069 "q-80896" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351187 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351187 1071 "What is the cardinality of $C\\times D$ for $C={0,1,2,...,11}$ and $D={s,t,u,v}?$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351187 1072 #uuid "6d75f887-72f7-5bce-8cad-02558c390258" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351187 1074 "For any two finite sets $C$ and $D,$ the cardinality of $C\\times D$ is\n\n$$\n|C\\times D|=|C|\\cdot |D|.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|C\\times D| & =|C|\\cdot |D| \\\\  & =12\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =48.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351188 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350895 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351188 1069 "q-80897" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351188 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351188 1071 "What is the cardinality of $K\\times M$ for $K={0,3,4,11,15}$ and $M={d,e,f}?$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351188 1072 #uuid "23d17fee-e14a-5091-a905-4d64eb61a108" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351188 1074 "For any two finite sets $K$ and $M,$ the cardinality of $K\\times M$ is\n\n$$\n|K\\times M|=|K|\\cdot |M|.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|K\\times M| & =|K|\\cdot |M| \\\\  & =5\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351189 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350772 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351189 1069 "q-8090" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351189 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351189 1071 "What linear transformation $T$ is represented by the following matrix\n$[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]?$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351189 1072 #uuid "a4ea07e2-2b18-5ed4-ac42-77d3595de881" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351189 1074 "We can find the images of the standard basis vectors $i$ and $j$ under the action of  $T,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(i) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=i \\\\ T(j) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]=5j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix represents a stretch of scale factor $5$ in the $y$-direction." 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351190 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350970 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351190 1069 "q-80910" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351190 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351190 1071 "Find the general solution to the matrix differential equation $\\,{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]x(t).$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351190 1072 #uuid "2fb1455a-7189-5b60-b173-cf0521e22458" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351190 1074 "Given a decoupled system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\lambda \\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\lambda t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the matrix of our differential equation is diagonal, the system is decoupled. \n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t/3}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t/3},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351191 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350863 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351191 1069 "q-80913" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351191 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351191 1071 "Find the general solution to the matrix differential equation $\\,{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{aligned}]x(t).$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351191 1072 #uuid "17dd9c7c-2bac-506a-ad25-7d2fd982cc45" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351191 1074 "Given a decoupled system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\lambda \\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\lambda t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the matrix of our differential equation is diagonal, the system is decoupled. \n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{10t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{10t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351192 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350761 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351192 1069 "q-80915" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351192 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351192 1071 "Consider the system of linear differential equations given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]x(t).\n$$\n\nGiven that the solution to the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ a\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ a\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-3t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351192 1072 #uuid "cae2c390-9a2f-587b-be5b-7ff082d424d2" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351192 1074 "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince $A$ is upper-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-3$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution of the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-3+3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -3+3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}0{w}_{1}+5{w}_{2}=1 \\\\ 0{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{2}=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{1}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{1}=0,$ we get \n\n$$\nw=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-3t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, we see that $a=0,b=\\frac{1}{5},$ and $a+b=\\frac{1}{5}.$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351193 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350805 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351193 1069 "q-80918" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351193 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351193 1071 "Consider the system of linear differential equations given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]x(t).\n$$\n\nGiven that the solution to the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{5t}+[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$\n\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of the system's matrix is $\\lambda =5.$*" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351193 1072 #uuid "ef21bab9-f763-5bd8-b177-dccd5650b9c2" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351193 1074 "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is $\\lambda =5$ (of multiplicity two). For this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution of the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}7-5 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 3-5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{w}_{1}-2{w}_{2}=1 \\\\ 2{w}_{1}-2{w}_{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the first equation is the same as the second. So, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}={w}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$ we get \n\n$$\nw=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{5t}+[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, we see that $a=1,b=\\frac{1}{2},$ and $a+b=\\frac{3}{2}.$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351194 1068 #edb/ref 17592186351130 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351194 1069 "q-80927" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351194 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351194 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351194 1072 #uuid "594f8d9b-4d22-56cb-9100-eca1eb140e4b" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351194 1074 "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince $A$ is upper-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-2$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-2+2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -2+2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}0{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2}=0 \\\\ 5{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$  we get\n\n$$\nw=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-2t}+[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{c}_{2}}{5} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{c}_{2}}{5} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-2t}+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}. \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{-2t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2t} \\\\ (2+5t){e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351195 1068 #edb/ref 17592186351048 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351195 1069 "q-80983" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351195 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351195 1071 "If the path $C$ is a part of the graph $y=\\sqrt{x}$ traversed from the point $A(0,0)$ to point $B(1,1),$ then  ${\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}+y)\\,dx+(3x+2y)\\,dy=$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351195 1072 #uuid "fd7813dc-b09d-5c4d-8539-6d09e01430cc" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351195 1074 "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y=\\sqrt{x}.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j={t}^{2}\\,i+t\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,1]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=2t\\,i+j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}+y)\\,dx+(3x+2y)\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+(3x+2y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(({x}^{2}+y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+(3x+2y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({({t}^{2})}^{2}+t)\\cdot 2t+(3{t}^{2}+2t)\\cdot 1\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{4}+t)\\cdot 2t+3{t}^{2}+2t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2{t}^{5}+5{t}^{2}+2t\\,dt \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{6}}{3}+\\frac{5{t}^{3}}{3}+{t}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\frac{(1)^{6}}{3}+\\frac{5(1)^{3}}{3}+(1)^{2}]-[0] \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{5}{3}+1]-[0] \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351196 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350785 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351196 1069 "q-8099" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351196 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351196 1071 "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-8y=6{e}^{4x}.\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351196 1072 #uuid "611b3314-877e-5323-b621-982dc2e7798e" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351196 1074 "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-8y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -8 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)(\\lambda -4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-2$ or $\\lambda =4$,  hence the complementary function ${y}_{c}$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $6{e}^{4x}.$ In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $4$, which coincides with one of the roots of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{4x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha (1+4x){e}^{4x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\alpha (8+16x){e}^{4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p},$ ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha (8+16x){e}^{4x}-2(\\alpha (1+4x){e}^{4x})-8\\alpha x{e}^{4x} & =6{e}^{4x} \\\\ (8+16x-2-8x-8x)\\alpha {e}^{4x} & =6{e}^{4x} \\\\ 6\\alpha {e}^{4x} & =6{e}^{4x} \\\\ 6\\alpha  & =6 \\\\ \\alpha  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=x{e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{4x}+x{e}^{4x}.\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351197 1068 #edb/ref 17592186351031 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351197 1069 "q-80996" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351197 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351197 1071 "Which of the following is a mathematical statement?" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351197 1072 #uuid "31109872-f63a-5337-8410-02fd12d8b091" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351197 1074 "A mathematical statement is a sentence or mathematical expression that is either true or false. \n\nOf the given sentences, the only sentence that is either true or false is \"$4$ is less than $1.$\" (This sentence is false.)\n\nAll the other sentences are neither true nor false." 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351198 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350819 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351198 1069 "q-8101" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351198 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351198 1071 "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+7\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+12y=21{e}^{3x}.\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351198 1072 #uuid "703bb75c-b102-5bd2-876e-82612d9b3036" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351198 1074 "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+7\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+12y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+7\\lambda +12 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)(\\lambda +4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-3$ or $\\lambda =-4$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{-4x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $21{e}^{3x}.$ In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $3$, which is not the root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=3\\alpha {e}^{3x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=9\\alpha {e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p}$, ${y}_{p}^{′}$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9\\alpha {e}^{3x}+7(3\\alpha {e}^{3x})+12(\\alpha {e}^{3x}) & =21{e}^{3x} \\\\ (9+21+12)\\alpha {e}^{3x} & =21{e}^{3x} \\\\ 42\\alpha  & =21 \\\\ \\alpha  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{-4x}+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{3x}.\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351199 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350892 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351199 1069 "q-8102" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351199 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351199 1071 "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{9}{4}y=6{e}^{3x/2}.\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351199 1072 #uuid "ff9e60ce-0c56-5edd-8dd6-d60ae5843660" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351199 1074 "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{9}{4}y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda +\\frac{9}{4} & =0 \\\\ {(\\lambda -\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =\\frac{3}{2}$ is a repeated root, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{3x/2}+Bx{e}^{3x/2}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $6{e}^{3x/2}.$ In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $\\frac{3}{2}$, which coincides with the double root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{3x/2},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha (2x+\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}){e}^{3x/2},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\alpha (2+6x+\\frac{9}{4}{x}^{2}){e}^{3x/2}.\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p},$ ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha (2+6x+\\frac{9}{4}{x}^{2}){e}^{3x/2}-3(\\alpha (2x+\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}){e}^{3x/2})+\\frac{9}{4}(\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{3x/2}) & =6{e}^{3x/2} \\\\ (2+6x+\\frac{9}{4}{x}^{2}-6x-\\frac{9}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{9}{4}{x}^{2})\\alpha {e}^{3x/2} & =6{e}^{3x/2} \\\\ 2\\alpha {e}^{3x/2} & =6{e}^{3x/2} \\\\ 2\\alpha  & =6 \\\\ \\alpha  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=3{x}^{2}{e}^{3x/2}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{3x/2}+Bx{e}^{3x/2}+3{x}^{2}{e}^{3x/2}.\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351200 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350912 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351200 1069 "q-81020" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351200 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351200 1071 "Compute the eigenvalues of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351200 1072 #uuid "4b89a585-8a35-5565-bf24-3655edb12ced" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351200 1074 "First, we find the matrix $\\,A-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(-1-\\lambda )-1(-2) & =0 \\\\ -1-\\lambda +\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+2 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-1 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =\\pm \\text{i}.$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351201 1068 #edb/ref 17592186351147 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351201 1069 "q-81022" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351201 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351201 1071 "Compute the eigenvalues of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351201 1072 #uuid "631640c2-885c-5c5e-bba0-18dfb668abd3" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351201 1074 "First, we find the matrix $\\,A-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-2(-1) & =0 \\\\ 1-2\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+2 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +3 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(-2)\\pm \\sqrt{(-2)^{2}-4(1)(3)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm \\sqrt{-8}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm 2\\text{i}\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =1\\pm \\text{i}\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =1\\pm \\text{i}\\sqrt{2}.$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351202 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350740 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351202 1069 "q-81023" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351202 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351202 1071 "Given that \n${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}2(x-1){y}^{2} & 2(x-1)^{2}y\\end{aligned}],$ \nevaluate the Hessian determinant of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(0,1).$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351202 1072 #uuid "0dab700a-fa4f-5675-9473-37a2b428ee20" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351202 1074 "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:{{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe Hessian determinant $H(x,y)$ is the determinant of the second derivative:\n\n$$\nH(x)=det({f}^{″}(x))\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}2(x-1){y}^{2} & 2(x-1)^{2}y\\end{aligned}],$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2(x-1){y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2(x-1)^{2}y.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2(x-1){y}^{2})=2{y}^{2}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2(x-1)^{2}y)=4(x-1)y \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2(x-1){y}^{2})=4(x-1)y\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2(x-1)^{2}y)=2(x-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2{y}^{2} & 4(x-1)y \\\\ 4(x-1)y & 2(x-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(0,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,1)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\nH(0,1)=2\\cdot 2-(-4)\\cdot (-4)=-12.\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351203 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350961 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351203 1069 "q-81024" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351203 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351203 1071 "Determine if $v=[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351203 1072 #uuid "edb2c398-ec60-50c0-bce1-beafe9c44e8e" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351203 1074 "The eigenvectors are non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 1+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-{\\text{i}}^{2}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i}(\\text{i}+1) \\\\ 1+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(1+\\text{i})[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(1+\\text{i})v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=(1+\\text{i})v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =1+\\text{i}.$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351204 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350866 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351204 1069 "q-81025" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351204 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351204 1071 "Determine if $v=[\\begin{aligned}1+\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 4 \\\\ -8 & 4\\end{aligned}].$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351204 1072 #uuid "46aa3476-303b-5222-af57-59b75f51d09f" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351204 1074 "The eigenvectors are non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 4 \\\\ -8 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1+\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4-4\\text{i} \\\\ -8\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4{\\text{i}}^{2}-4\\text{i} \\\\ -8\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-4\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}1+\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-4\\text{i}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=-4\\text{i}v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-4\\text{i}.$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351205 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350807 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351205 1069 "q-81027" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351205 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351205 1071 "If $v=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2+\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351205 1072 #uuid "7207a8c6-6c0f-53ed-83a0-48cf577c32a8" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351205 1074 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-2+\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-2+\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3-\\text{i} & -2 \\\\ 5 & 3-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(-3-\\text{i}){x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}+(3-\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(-2+\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{5}(3-\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{5}(3-\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-5,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}3-\\text{i} \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{-5}{3-\\text{i}}=\\frac{-5(3+\\text{i})}{10}=-\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}.$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351206 1068 #edb/ref 17592186351112 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351206 1069 "q-8103" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351206 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351206 1071 "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-5\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{25y}{4}=4{e}^{-x}.\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351206 1072 #uuid "5ad9bde6-0f28-5d02-9140-ff3f90ecfdc9" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351206 1074 "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-5\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{25y}{4}=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda +\\frac{25}{4} & =0 \\\\ {(\\lambda -\\frac{5}{2})}^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =\\frac{5}{2}$ is a repeated root, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{5x/2}+Bx{e}^{5x/2}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $4{e}^{-x}.$ In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $-1$, which is not the root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-\\alpha {e}^{-x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\alpha {e}^{-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p},$ ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha {e}^{-x}-5(-\\alpha {e}^{-x})+\\frac{25}{4}\\alpha {e}^{-x} & =4{e}^{-x} \\\\ (1+5+\\frac{25}{4})\\alpha {e}^{-x} & =4{e}^{-x} \\\\ \\frac{49}{4}\\alpha {e}^{-x} & =4{e}^{-x} \\\\ \\frac{49}{4}\\alpha  & =4 \\\\ \\alpha  & =\\frac{16}{49}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{16}{49}{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{5x/2}+Bx{e}^{5x/2}+\\frac{16}{49}{e}^{-x}.\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351207 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350749 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351207 1069 "q-81030" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351207 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351207 1071 "If $v=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ 13 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1+3\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351207 1072 #uuid "bcaaf961-40fb-5a48-94d0-be1d8933da54" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351207 1074 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-1+3\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ 13 & 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-1+3\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1+3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2-3\\text{i} & -1 \\\\ 13 & 2-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(-2-3\\text{i}){x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 13{x}_{1}+(2-3\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(-1+3\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=(-2-3\\text{i}){x}_{1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ -(2+3\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=-1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2+3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{-1}{2+3\\text{i}}=\\frac{-(2-3\\text{i})}{13}=-\\frac{2}{13}+\\frac{3}{13}\\text{i}.$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351208 1068 #edb/ref 17592186351122 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351208 1069 "q-81031" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351208 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351208 1071 "If $v=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-\\sqrt{2}\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351208 1072 #uuid "d7f8f4ed-a707-5ef8-a488-4f074f1c04ff" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351208 1074 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-\\sqrt{2}\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2}\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i} & 2 \\\\ -3 & -2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}){x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+(-2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(-\\sqrt{2}\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}(2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}){x}_{1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}(2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=-2,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{-2}{2+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}}=\\frac{-2(2-\\sqrt{2}\\text{i})}{6}=-\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\text{i}.$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351209 1068 #edb/ref 1759218635081