}) \\\\  & =6\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351211 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350821 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351211 1069 "q-81036" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351211 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351211 1071 "Given that ${\\lambda }_{1}=6-\\text{i}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}$ are eigenvalues of a real $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$, find $3({\\lambda }_{1}+{\\lambda }_{2}).$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351211 1072 #uuid "7f581ea3-262c-5a81-aae2-3087d78c564b" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351211 1074 "Since ${\\lambda }_{1}=6-\\text{i}$ is a complex eigenvalue of $A,$ the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the complex conjugate of ${\\lambda }_{1}.$\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}=6+\\text{i}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3({\\lambda }_{1}+{\\lambda }_{2}) & =3(6-\\text{i}+(6+\\text{i})) \\\\  & =3(12) \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351212 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350899 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351212 1069 "q-81037" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351212 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351212 1071 "Given that ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1}\\end{aligned}]$ and ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ are linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 5 \\\\ -5 & -3\\end{aligned}],$\nwhat is the value of $\\frac{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}?$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351212 1072 #uuid "0f9d51e4-0acf-5ee0-b7ae-a69128fd9881" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351212 1074 "First, we need to find the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we must solve the characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}5-\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ -5 & -3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )(-3-\\lambda )-(-5)(5) & =0 \\\\ -15-5\\lambda +3\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+25 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +10 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(-2)\\pm \\sqrt{(-2)^{2}-4(1)(10)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(-2)\\pm \\sqrt{-36}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm 6\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =1\\pm 3\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =1\\pm 3\\text{i}.$\n\nNow, let's consider the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=1+3\\text{i}.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 5 \\\\ -5 & -3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1+3\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1+3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4-3\\text{i} & 5 \\\\ -5 & -4-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(4-3\\text{i}){x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -5{x}_{1}+(-4-3\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(1+3\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{5}(-4-3\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}(-4-3\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-5,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}4+3\\text{i} \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, to compute the second eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=1-3\\text{i}$, we simply compute the complex conjugate of ${\\overset{―}{v}}_{1},$ and get\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}4-3\\text{i} \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}} & =\\frac{(-5)(-5)}{(4+3\\text{i})(4-3\\text{i})}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351213 1068 #edb/ref 17592186351118 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351213 1069 "q-81043" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351213 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351213 1071 "Find and categorize all local the extrema of the function $f(x,y)={e}^{x}-x+{y}^{2},$ and determine which of the following statements is true." 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351213 1072 #uuid "6d35a3af-0061-562b-9545-45ddd0993045" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351213 1074 "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f′(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x}-x+{y}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x}-x+{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}-1 & 2y\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting $f′(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}-1=0 \\\\ 2y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $x$, we get\n\n$$\n{e}^{x}-1=0\\,⟹\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n2y=0\\,⟹\\,y=0.\n$$\n\nHence, $(0,0)$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y).$  \n\nTo classify this stationary point, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x}-1)={e}^{x}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y)=0 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x}-1)=0\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative  is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(0,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(0,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(0,0)=det({f}^{″}(0,0))=1\\cdot 2-{0}^{2}=2>0.\n$$\n\nFinally, notice that \n\n$$\n\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(0,0)=({f}^{″}(0,0))_{11}=1>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second partial derivative test, we have that $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(0,0).$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351214 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350969 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351214 1069 "q-81059" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351214 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351214 1071 "What is the coefficient of ${(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}$ in the second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}?$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351214 1072 #uuid "0b1df791-ae8a-5761-94bd-f7868c7dce6d" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351214 1074 "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =f(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+{f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}){(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient of ${(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}$ in the above Taylor polynomial is \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}).\n$$\n\nTo find this coefficient, we start by computing the second derivative ${f}^{″}(x):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${f}^{″}(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine the coefficient of ${(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351215 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350788 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351215 1069 "q-81060" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351215 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351215 1071 "What is the coefficient of $(x-\\pi )^{2}$ in the second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ at $x=\\pi ?$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351215 1072 #uuid "5c0d84c6-16f1-535d-9a68-5f9a72b2e4a8" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351215 1074 "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=\\pi $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =f(\\pi )+{f}^{′}(\\pi )(x-\\pi )+\\frac{{f}^{″}(\\pi )}{2}(x-\\pi )^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient of $(x-\\pi )^{2}$ in the above Taylor polynomial is \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo find this coefficient, we start by computing the second derivative ${f}^{″}(x):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${f}^{″}(x)$ at $x=\\pi .$ This gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(\\pi )=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )=-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine the coefficient of $(x-\\pi )^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{f}^{″}(\\pi )}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351216 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350841 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351216 1069 "q-81062" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351216 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351216 1071 "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of a function $f(x)$ about $x=-3$ is given by ${P}_{2}(x)=-24+36(x+3)-18(x+3)^{2}.$ What is the value of ${f}^{″}(-3)?$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351216 1072 #uuid "55407140-1d53-5c07-ab9b-fd1092266d7c" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351216 1074 "This is an expansion about the point $x=-3,$ so the coefficient of the second-degree term is $\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(-3).$ \n\nIn the given equation, we see that the coefficient of the second-degree term is $-18.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(-3)=-18\\,⟹\\,{f}^{″}(-3)=-36.\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351217 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350831 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351217 1069 "q-81063" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351217 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351217 1071 "![](images/e4/e483403ebe85920111334dacfc22dc907ab3c33cd38b33361d7634132032c811.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following could be the second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f$ about $x=1?$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351217 1072 #uuid "43e92da4-120e-5f12-8aab-f38c3afaf5f3" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351217 1074 "The second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f(x)$ about $x=1$ is \n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f(1)+{f}^{′}(1)(x-1)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(1)}{2}(x-1)^{2}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we observe:\n\n- $f(1)<0.$ Therefore, the first term of the Taylor polynomial must be negative.\n\n- ${f}^{′}(1)<0,$ because the slope of the tangent at $x=1$ is negative. Therefore, the coefficient of the second term in the Taylor polynomial must be negative.\n\n- ${f}^{″}(1)>0,$ because the graph is concave up at $x=1.$ Therefore, the coefficient of the third term in the Taylor polynomial must be positive.\n\nThe only option that meets the above criteria is\n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=-5-2(x-1)+6(x-1)^{2}.\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351218 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350777 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351218 1069 "q-81064" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351218 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351218 1071 "![](images/18/185b5924c542bb9f396fe826aac141256a13b967c45c47afc87ca07445df062f.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following could be the second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f$ about $x=-1?$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351218 1072 #uuid "0d3ec608-2690-560e-ab12-4c63cb591926" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351218 1074 "The second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f(x)$ about $x=-1$ is \n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f(-1)+{f}^{′}(-1)(x+1)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(-1)}{2}(x+1)^{2}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we observe:\n\n- $f(-1)>0.$ Therefore, the first term of the Taylor polynomial must be positive.\n\n- ${f}^{′}(-1)>0,$ because the slope of the tangent at $x=-1$ is positive. Therefore, the coefficient of the second term in the Taylor polynomial must be positive.\n\n- ${f}^{″}(-1)<0,$ because the graph is concave down at $x=-1.$ Therefore, the coefficient of the third term in the Taylor polynomial must be negative.\n\nThe only option that meets the above criteria is\n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=6+4(x+1)-5(x+1)^{2}.\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351219 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350738 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351219 1069 "q-8108" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351219 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351219 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+10y=-6{e}^{-2x},\\,y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})={e}^{-\\pi },\\text{ }\\text{ }{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=-2{e}^{-\\pi }.\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351219 1072 #uuid "0a7f18b4-bfd2-5978-b5ec-b53743924e99" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351219 1074 "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+10y=0.\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +10=0.\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +10 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +9)-9+10 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ \\lambda +3 & =\\pm \\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-3\\pm \\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{c}(x) & ={e}^{-3x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $-6{e}^{-2x}.$ In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $-2$, which is not the root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{-2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-2\\alpha {e}^{-2x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=4\\alpha {e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p},$ ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\alpha {e}^{-2x}+6(-2\\alpha {e}^{-2x})+10(\\alpha {e}^{-2x}) & =-6{e}^{-2x} \\\\ (4-12+10)\\alpha {e}^{-2x} & =-6{e}^{-2x} \\\\ 2\\alpha  & =-6 \\\\ \\alpha  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-3{e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-3x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-3{e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nNow let's find the constants $P$ and $Q.$ \n\nSubstituting $y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})={e}^{-\\pi }$ into the general solution gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q{e}^{-3\\pi /2}-3{e}^{-\\pi } & ={e}^{-\\pi } \\\\ Q{e}^{-3\\pi /2} & =4{e}^{-\\pi } \\\\ Q & =4{e}^{\\pi /2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=-2{e}^{-\\pi }.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(Q-3P){e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-(P+3Q){e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+6{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=-2{e}^{-\\pi }$ into the above gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-(P+3Q){e}^{-3\\pi /2}+6{e}^{-\\pi } & =-2{e}^{-\\pi } \\\\ -(P+12{e}^{\\pi /2}){e}^{-3\\pi /2} & =-8{e}^{-\\pi } \\\\ -P{e}^{-3\\pi /2}-12{e}^{-\\pi } & =-8{e}^{-\\pi } \\\\ -P{e}^{-3\\pi /2} & =4{e}^{-\\pi } \\\\ P & =-4{e}^{\\pi /2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $P=-4{e}^{\\pi /2}$ and $Q=4{e}^{\\pi /2}.$\n\nWe substitute this into the general particular integral and find that the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(-4{e}^{\\pi /2}){e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+(4{e}^{\\pi /2}){e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3{e}^{-2x} \\\\ y & =4{e}^{-3x+\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-3{e}^{-2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351220 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350981 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351220 1069 "q-8109" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351220 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351220 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-4{y}^{′}+8y=20{e}^{2x},\\,y(0)=1,\\text{ }\\text{ }{y}^{′}(0)=2.\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351220 1072 #uuid "0c1cbd4b-2b58-54b2-8818-d30f2297ce38" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351220 1074 "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-4{y}^{′}+8y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +8=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +8 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +4)-4+8 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)^{2} & =-4 \\\\ \\lambda -2 & =\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =2\\pm 2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{c}(x) & ={e}^{2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $20{e}^{2x}.$ In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $2,$ which is not the root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha {e}^{2x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=4\\alpha {e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p},$ ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\alpha {e}^{2x}-4(2\\alpha {e}^{2x})+8(\\alpha {e}^{2x}) & =20{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\alpha (4-8+8){e}^{2x} & =20{e}^{2x} \\\\ 4\\alpha {e}^{2x} & =20{e}^{2x} \\\\ 4\\alpha  & =20 \\\\ \\alpha  & =5\\text{ }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=5{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)+5{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nNow let's find the constants $P$ and $Q.$ \n\nSubstituting $y(0)=1$ into the general solution gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=2.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2{e}^{2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)+{e}^{2x}(-2P\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+2Q\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)+10{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=2$ into the above gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2P+2Q+10 & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $P=-4$ and $Q=0.$\n\nWe substitute this into the general particular integral and find that the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={e}^{2x}(-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+0)+5{e}^{2x} \\\\  & =-4{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+5{e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351221 1068 #edb/ref 17592186351045 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351221 1069 "q-81097" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351221 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351221 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}-y\\,dx+x\\,dy,$ where $C$ is a portion of the circle parametrized by $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,\\pi ].$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351221 1072 #uuid "346018a5-76ad-5ea5-a1fa-213af5e9b80a" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351221 1074 "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text