int }_{0}^{2}2xy\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+x\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(2xy\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+x\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2t({t}^{2}-t)\\cdot 1+t(2t-1)\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2{t}^{3}-t\\,dt \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{4}}{2}-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{{2}^{4}}{2}-\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2}]-[0] \\\\  & =8-2 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351223 1068 #edb/ref 17592186351002 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351223 1069 "q-8110" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351223 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351223 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}-5y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x,\\,y(0)=-\\frac{2}{5},\\,{y}^{′}(0)=\\frac{1}{10}.\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351223 1072 #uuid "77dcab2f-d394-5343-acb3-cfbabfe50eb4" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351223 1074 "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}-5y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda -5 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +5)(\\lambda -1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-5$ or $\\lambda =1$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-5x}+B{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.$ Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x \\\\ {y}^{″} & =-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+4(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x)-5(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives:\n\n$$\n(-14\\alpha +12\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+(-14\\beta -12\\alpha )\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-14\\alpha +12\\beta =1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x) \\\\ -14\\beta -12\\alpha =-4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{1}{10},\\beta =\\frac{1}{5}.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-5x}+B{e}^{x}+\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nNow let us find constants $A$ and $B.$ \n\nSubstituting $y(0)=-\\frac{2}{5}$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nA+B=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=\\frac{1}{10}.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-5A{e}^{-5x}+B{e}^{x}-\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=\\frac{1}{10}$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n-5A+B=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations for $A$ and $B$:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}A+B=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -5A+B=-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=0,$ $B=-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}+\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351224 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350923 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351224 1069 "q-81102" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351224 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351224 1071 "If the path $C$ is the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from the point $A(0,1)$ to the point $B(2,3),$ then ${\\int }_{C}{e}^{x}\\,dx-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi y}{2})\\,dy=$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351224 1072 #uuid "88864edb-1c9c-588d-bdc6-1eef5e251f66" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351224 1074 "The position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=j$ and $b=2\\,i+3\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =j+t(2\\,i+3\\,j-j) \\\\  & =j+t(2\\,i+2j) \\\\  & =2t\\,i+(1+2t)\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x(t)=2t$ and $y(t)=1+2t$ along the segment.\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=2\\,i+2\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{e}^{x}\\,dx-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi y}{2})\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{x}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi y}{2})\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{x}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi y}{2})\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{2t}\\cdot 2-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi (1+2t)}{2})\\cdot 2\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2{e}^{2t}-2\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi t))dt \\\\  & ={[{e}^{2t}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi t)]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[{e}^{2(1)}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi (1))]-[{e}^{2(0)}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi (0))] \\\\  & =[{e}^{2}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})]-[1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})] \\\\  & =[{e}^{2}+0]-[1+0] \\\\  & ={e}^{2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351225 1068 #edb/ref 17592186351078 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351225 1069 "q-81103" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351225 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351225 1071 "Given the orthogonal basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the vector $x$ below, find the value of $15{x}_{1},$ where ${x}_{1}$ is the first component of $[x]_{B}.$\n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]},\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351225 1072 #uuid "a0ea9087-dcb2-563d-a61a-22e857198678" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351225 1074 "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${q}_{1},\\,{q}_{2}.$ \n\nSince $B$ is an orthogonal basis, we have that $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$, where ${x}_{i}=\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{i}}{{q}_{i}\\cdot {q}_{i}}.$ Computing ${x}_{1},$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{1}}{{q}_{1}\\cdot {q}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 1+(-4)\\cdot (-3)}{{1}^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2+12}{1+9} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n15{x}_{1}=15\\cdot \\frac{7}{5}=21.\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351226 1068 #edb/ref 17592186351067 13194139533430 175