 \n\nSince $B$ is an orthogonal basis, we have that $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$, where ${x}_{i}=\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{i}}{{q}_{i}\\cdot {q}_{i}}.$ Computing ${x}_{2},$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{2}}{{q}_{2}\\cdot {q}_{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\cdot 3+(-3)\\cdot (-1)}{{3}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{15+3}{9+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n10{x}_{2}=10\\cdot \\frac{9}{5}=18.\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351228 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350830 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351228 1069 "q-81106" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351228 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351228 1071 "Given the **orthonormal** basis $B={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]}$ of ${R}^{2},$ find $[x]_{B}$ if $x=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351228 1072 #uuid "820db9dd-f4cd-512d-b911-c52d62f6eb3f" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351228 1074 "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${q}_{1},\\,{q}_{2}.$ \n\nSince $B$ is an orthonormal basis, we have that $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$, where ${x}_{i}=x\\cdot {q}_{i}.$ Computing the components of $x,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]=-\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{10}}-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{5}}=-\\frac{11}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ {x}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]=-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}}+\\frac{10}{\\sqrt{10}}=\\frac{7}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{11}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{7}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351229 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350986 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351229 1069 "q-81109" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351229 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351229 1071 "Which of the following sets are orthonormal?\n\n- $U={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $V={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $W={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}]}$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351229 1072 #uuid "25a9c5a2-c2b4-580a-a454-817fa98a8c5d" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351229 1074 "Let's denote the vectors of the given sets as \n${{u}_{1},{u}_{2},{u}_{3}},$ \n${{v}_{1},{v}_{2}},$ and \n${{w}_{1},{w}_{2}},$ respectively. \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthonormal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0 \\\\ {u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}=1 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}=1 \\\\ {u}_{3}\\cdot {u}_{3}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}=1 \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=0 \\\\ {w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{3}=0 \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1}=1 \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{2}=1 \\\\ {w}_{3}\\cdot {w}_{3}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=1+25+4=30\\ne 1 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $U$ is not an orthonormal set.\n\n- For the set $V,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]=-\\frac{6}{13}+\\frac{6}{13}=0 & ✓ \\\\ {v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{4}{13}+\\frac{9}{13}=1 & ✓ \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{9}{13}+\\frac{4}{13}=1 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $V$ is orthonormal.\n\n- For the set $W,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{18}}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{18}}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{18}}=0 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{4}{6}+\\frac{1}{6}=1 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}=1 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $W$ is orthonormal.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"$V$ and $W$ only\"." 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351230 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350962 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351230 1069 "q-81110" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351230 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351230 1071 "Find ${f}^{″}(x,y)$ for the function $f(x,y)$ given that\n${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}e-{e}^{2-x} & 2y\\end{aligned}]$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351230 1072 #uuid "5fc8ae5c-d352-5fd3-ae18-630a629fa87e" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351230 1074 "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:{{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}e-{e}^{2-x} & 2y\\end{aligned}],$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=e-{e}^{2-x},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2y.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(e-{e}^{2-x})={e}^{2-x}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y)=0 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(e-{e}^{2-x})=0\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2-x} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351231 1068 #edb/ref 17592186351145 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351231 1069 "q-81111" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351231 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351231 1071 "Find ${f}^{″}(x,y)$ for the function $f(x,y)={e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351231 1072 #uuid "9e5fb489-dfa6-50c2-a270-dc2d65f25d4f" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351231 1074 "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:{{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & 2y{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=(2+4{x}^{2}){e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=4xy{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=4xy{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=(2+4{y}^{2}){e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}(2+4{x}^{2}){e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & 4xy{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\ 4xy{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & (2+4{y}^{2}){e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351232 1068 #edb/ref 17592186351038 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351232 1069 "q-81114" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351232 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351232 1071 "Consider the **orthonormal** set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $x=[{x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2}]^{T},$ extend the set so that it forms an **orthonormal** basis of ${R}^{2}.$ What is the value of $|{x}_{1}|+|{x}_{2}|?$\n\n$$\nU={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]}\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351232 1072 #uuid "69162096-003d-5b5d-8f8d-d7d2695037b5" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351232 1074 "Let's denote the vector of $U$ as ${u}_{1}.$ To extend the given set to an orthonormal basis of ${R}^{2}$, we need to find a second vector $x$ such that $‖x‖=1,$ and\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot x=0\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{2}=0.\n$$\n\nFrom the above equation, we get ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we obtain the vector \n$x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nNow, we need to normalize $x.$ Notice that $‖x‖=\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\sqrt{2}.$ So, we normalize $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{‖x‖}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthonormal basis of ${R}^{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $|{x}_{1}|+|{x}_{2}|=|-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}|+|\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}|=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}}=\\sqrt{2}.$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351233 1068 #edb/ref 17592186351140 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351233 1069 "q-81115" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351233 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351233 1071 "Consider the **orthonormal** set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $x=[{x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2}]^{T},$ extend the set so that it forms an **orthonormal** basis of ${R}^{2}.$ What is the value of $|{x}_{1}|+|{x}_{2}|?$\n\n$$\nU={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]}\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351233 1072 #uuid "9eae85b5-72a3-53d6-aeb1-1aeb5a7806f5" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351233 1074 "Let's denote the vector of $U$ as ${u}_{1}.$ To extend the given set to an orthonormal basis of ${R}^{2}$, we need to find a second vector $x$ such that $‖x‖=1,$ and\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot x=0\\,⟹\\,\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{2}=0.\n$$\n\nFrom the above equation, we get ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-2,$ we obtain the vector \n$x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nNow, we need to normalize $x.$ Notice that $‖x‖=\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}=\\sqrt{5}.$ So, we normalize $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{‖x‖}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthonormal basis of ${R}^{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $|{x}_{1}|+|{x}_{2}|=|\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}|+|-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}|=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}}.$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351234 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350928 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351234 1069 "q-81117" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351234 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351234 1071 "Consider the **orthonormal** set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $x=[{x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}]^{T},$ extend the set so that it forms an **orthonormal** basis of ${R}^{3}.$ What is the value of $|{x}_{1}|+|{x}_{2}|+|{x}_{3}|?$\n\n$$\nU={[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}\\end{aligned}]}\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351234 1072 #uuid "7ee661be-3380-50f2-9670-d857c4eed458" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351234 1074 "Let's denote the vectors of $U$ as ${u}_{1},\\,{u}_{2}.$ To extend the given set to an orthonormal basis of ${R}^{3}$, we need to find a third vector $x$ such that $‖x‖=1,$ and\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot x=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot x=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}{x}_{2}+\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}}{x}_{2}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system above and reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}} & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=\\sqrt{10}\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=\\sqrt{14}\\cdot {R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=3{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n-2{x}_{1}+3(3{x}_{3})+{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=5{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{3} \\\\ 3{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=1,$ we obtain the vector \n$x=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nNow, we need to normalize $x.$ Notice that $‖x‖=\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\sqrt{35}.$ So, we normalize $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{‖x‖}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{35}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{35}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{35}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthonormal basis of ${R}^{3}$ is\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{35}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{35}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{35}}\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $|{x}_{1}|+|{x}_{2}|+|{x}_{3}|=|\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{35}}|+|\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{35}}|+|\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{35}}|=\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{35}}.$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351235 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350755 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351235 1069 "q-81119" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351235 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351235 1071 "Find ${f}^{″}(x,y)$ for the function $f(x,y)={y}^{2}({x}^{2}-1).$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351235 1072 #uuid "235e87ab-40f0-5bca-9e1d-4479ce5e2de8" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351235 1074 "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:{{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}({x}^{2}-1)) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}({x}^{2}-1))\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2x{y}^{2} & 2y({x}^{2}-1)\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x{y}^{2})=2{y}^{2}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y({x}^{2}-1))=4xy \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x{y}^{2})=4xy\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y({x}^{2}-1))=2({x}^{2}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}2{y}^{2} & 4xy \\\\ 4xy & 2({x}^{2}-1)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351236 1068 #edb/ref 17592186351035 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351236 1069 "q-81123" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351236 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351236 1071 "Find ${f}^{″}(-1,2)$ for the function $f(x,y)=(y-1)({x}^{2}-1).$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351236 1072 #uuid "e0469958-83f9-5c79-aae7-bbff4a96354f" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351236 1074 "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:{{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}((y-1)({x}^{2}-1)) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}((y-1)({x}^{2}-1))\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2x(y-1) & {x}^{2}-1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x(y-1))=2(y-1)\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-1)=2x \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x(y-1))=2x\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}2(y-1) & 2x \\\\ 2x & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate this matrix at $(-1,2).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-1,2)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351237 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350979 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351237 1069 "q-81124" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351237 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351237 1071 "Let $a=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Find the orthogonal projection of $a$ onto $\\text{Span}{b}.$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351237 1072 #uuid "3612a3cb-afdb-5015-8c9f-45f1c24e669c" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351237 1074 "The orthogonal projection of $a$ onto $\\text{Span}{b}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{b}\\,a & =\\frac{b\\cdot a}{b\\cdot b}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 2+0\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 5}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}}b \\\\  & =\\frac{2+2+0+5}{1+1+0+1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{3}b \\\\  & =3b \\\\  & =3[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351238 1068 #edb/ref 17592186351103 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351238 1069 "q-8113" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351238 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351238 1071 "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{y}{4}=25\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351238 1072 #uuid "19bd44fa-2632-53a7-82f0-7e7751665f68" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351238 1074 "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $25\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+\\frac{\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{4}=25\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{3}{4}\\alpha -\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+(\\alpha -\\frac{3}{4}\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=25\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{4}\\alpha -\\beta =25\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ \\alpha -\\frac{3}{4}\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-12$ and $\\beta =-16.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-12\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351239 1068 #edb/ref 17592186351030 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351239 1069 "q-81137" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351239 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351239 1071 "Given the orthogonal basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the vector $x$ below, find the value of $30{x}_{1},$ where ${x}_{1}$ is the first component of $[x]_{B}.$\n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]},\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351239 1072 #uuid "85ad24bc-d7fc-5a59-9ed0-5c084155a81f" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351239 1074 "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${q}_{1},\\,{q}_{2}.$ \n\nSince $B$ is an orthogonal basis, we have that $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$, where ${x}_{i}=\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{i}}{{q}_{i}\\cdot {q}_{i}}.$ Computing ${x}_{1},$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{1}}{{q}_{1}\\cdot {q}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 2}{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3+4}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n30{x}_{1}=30\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}=6.\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351240 1068 #edb/ref 17592186351066 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351240 1069 "q-81141" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351240 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351240 1071 "Given the **orthonormal** basis $B$ of ${R}^{3}$ and the vector $x$ below, find the value of $\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{1}$, where ${x}_{1}$ is the first component of $[x]_{B}.$\n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{35}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{35}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{35}}\\end{aligned}]},\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{10} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351240 1072 #uuid "e6504cce-f8c4-5ad3-a096-ce35234289bd" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351240 1074 "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${q}_{1},\\,{q}_{2},\\,{q}_{3}.$ \n\nSince $B$ is an orthonormal basis, we have that $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]$, where ${x}_{i}=x\\cdot {q}_{i}.$ Computing ${x}_{1},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =x\\cdot {q}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{10} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-2)=-1.\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351241 1068 #edb/ref 17592186351143 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351241 1069 "q-81142" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351241 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351241 1071 "Given the **orthonormal** basis $B$ of ${R}^{3}$ and the vector $x$ below, find the value of $\\sqrt{6}{x}_{2}$, where ${x}_{2}$ is the second component of $[x]_{B}.$\n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{30}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{30}} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{30}}\\end{aligned}]},\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351241 1072 #uuid "2c7b8cdd-da80-5fc5-a44b-0a5962fa099a" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351241 1074 "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${q}_{1},\\,{q}_{2},\\,{q}_{3}.$ \n\nSince $B$ is an orthonormal basis, we have that $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]$, where ${x}_{i}=x\\cdot {q}_{i}.$ Computing ${x}_{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =x\\cdot {q}_{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{6}}+\\frac{10}{\\sqrt{6}}-\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\sqrt{6}{x}_{2}=\\sqrt{6}\\cdot \\sqrt{6}=6.\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351242 1068 #edb/ref 17592186351116 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351242 1069 "q-81144" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351242 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351242 1071 "Compute the eigenvalues of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -17 & -1\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351242 1072 #uuid "489407c0-2411-5284-9004-ca7866bd0a3b" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351242 1074 "First, we find the matrix $\\,A-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ -17 & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ -17 & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(-1-\\lambda )-1(-17) & =0 \\\\ -1-\\lambda +\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+17 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+16 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-16 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm 4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =\\pm 4\\text{i}.$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351243 1068 #edb/ref 17592186351142 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351243 1069 "q-81145" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351243 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351243 1071 "Compute the eigenvalues of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 13 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351243 1072 #uuid "07c8f901-7555-5b51-8d26-5da48a69d081" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351243 1074 "First, we find the matrix $\\,A-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}5-\\lambda  & 13 \\\\ -1 & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}5-\\lambda  & 13 \\\\ -1 & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )(-1-\\lambda )-13(-1) & =0 \\\\ -5-5\\lambda +\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+13 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +8 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(-4)\\pm \\sqrt{(-4)^{2}-4(1)(8)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pm \\sqrt{-16}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pm 4\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =2\\pm 2\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =2\\pm 2\\text{i}.$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351244 1068 #edb/ref 17592186351029 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351244 1069 "q-8115" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351244 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351244 1071 "Find the solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y=13\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351244 1072 #uuid "cca5fd1d-b766-5deb-a0cc-75730b11009e" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351244 1074 "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +4 & =0 \\\\ {(\\lambda -2)}^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =2$ is a repeated root, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{2x}+Bx{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $13\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x} & =-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)-4(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x)+4(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)=13\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(-5\\alpha -12\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+(12\\alpha -5\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x=13\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-5\\alpha -12\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x) \\\\ 12\\alpha -5\\beta =13\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{12}{13}$ and $\\beta =-\\frac{5}{13}.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{12}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-\\frac{5}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{2x}+Bx{e}^{2x}+\\frac{12}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-\\frac{5}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351245 1068 #edb/ref 17592186351040 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351245 1069 "q-81157" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351245 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351245 1071 "Find the fourth-degree Taylor polynomial of ${sin}^{2}⁡(x)$ about $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351245 1072 #uuid "3169dc8c-e50f-57c2-bbce-69548992f510" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351245 1074 "The fourth-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=a$ is given by\n\n$$\n{P}_{4}(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(a)}{3!}(x-a)^{3}+\\frac{{f}^{(4)}(a)}{4!}(x-a)^{4}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $a=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ so\n\n$$\n{P}_{4}(x)=f(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+{f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{{f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{2!}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{3!}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{3}+\\frac{{f}^{(4)}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{4!}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{4}.\n$$\n\nSo we need to work out $f(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ ${f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ ${f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ ${f}^{‴}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and ${f}^{(4)}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={sin}^{2}⁡(x) & ⟹ &  & f(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & ={sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) & ⟹ &  & {f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{2})=0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & ⟹ &  & {f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{2})=-2 \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) & ⟹ &  & {f}^{‴}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{2})=0 \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(x) & =-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & ⟹ &  & {f}^{(4)}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{2})=8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${P}_{4}(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{4}(x) & =1+(0)(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})+(\\frac{(-2)}{2!}){(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}+\\frac{0}{3!}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{3}+\\frac{8}{4!}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{4} \\\\  & =1-{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351246 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350834 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351246 1069 "q-81159" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351246 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351246 1071 "The fourth-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=2$ is given by\n\n$$\n{P}_{4}(x)=1+2(x-2)+2(x-2)^{2}+\\frac{4}{3}(x-2)^{3}+\\frac{2}{3}(x-2)^{4}.\n$$\n\nWhat is ${f}^{‴}(2)?$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351246 1072 #uuid "cb643eec-f56f-5d2c-9e97-9166528177fa" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351246 1074 "The coefficient of $(x-2)^{3}$ is given by $\\frac{{f}^{‴}(2)}{3!}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{f}^{‴}(2)}{3!} & =\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ \\frac{{f}^{‴}(2)}{6} & =\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(2) & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351247 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350737 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351247 1069 "q-8116" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351247 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351247 1071 "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+10y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+31\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351247 1072 #uuid "23d5837f-a5f5-540d-89f5-9d0e45090f06" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351247 1074 "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+10y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +10 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +1)-1+10 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2} & =-9 \\\\ \\lambda +1 & =\\pm 3\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-1\\pm 3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-1\\pm 3\\text{i}$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}={e}^{-x}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x).\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+31\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x} & =-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)+2(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x)+10(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+31\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +6\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+(-6\\alpha +\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+31\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}\\alpha +6\\beta =1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x) \\\\ -6\\alpha +\\beta =31\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-5$ and $\\beta =1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-x}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351248 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350719 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351248 1069 "q-81161" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351248 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351248 1071 "The fourth-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=2$ is given by\n\n$$\n{P}_{4}(x)=-4-12(x-2)-5(x-2)^{2}-2(x-2)^{3}+(x-2)^{4}.\n$$\n\nWhat is ${f}^{(4)}(2)?$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351248 1072 #uuid "c5c7754c-03ee-5432-a2a9-6feca3310847" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351248 1074 "The coefficient of $(x-2)^{4}$ is given by $\\frac{{f}^{(4)}(2)}{4!}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{f}^{(4)}(2)}{4!} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{f}^{(4)}(2)}{24} & =1 \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(2) & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351249 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350826 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351249 1069 "q-81162" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351249 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351249 1071 "The fifth-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=2$ is given by\n\n$$\n{P}_{5}(x)=(x-2)-\\frac{1}{2}(x-2)^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}(x-2)^{3}-\\frac{1}{4}(x-2)^{4}+\\frac{1}{5}(x-2)^{5}.\n$$\n\nWhat is ${f}^{(4)}(2)?$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351249 1072 #uuid "28c4998d-617f-5445-bad5-02fef1782dd3" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351249 1074 "The coefficient of $(x-2)^{4}$ is given by $\\frac{{f}^{(4)}(2)}{4!}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{f}^{(4)}(2)}{4!} & =-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{{f}^{(4)}(2)}{24} & =-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(2) & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351250 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350905 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351250 1069 "q-81164" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351250 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351250 1071 "The function \n$f(x,y)$ \nhas the second derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}y{e}^{x} & {e}^{x}-{e}^{y} \\\\ {e}^{x}-{e}^{y} & -x{e}^{y}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(1,1).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $H(1,1)>0$\n- $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,1)>0$\n- The point $(1,1)$ is a local maximum of $f$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351250 1072 #uuid "5521ee3b-fffd-5558-98b1-b81f3a08c9ea" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351250 1074 "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,1)=[\\begin{aligned}e & 0 \\\\ 0 & -e\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(1,1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(1,1)=det({f}^{″}(1,1))=e\\cdot (-e)-{0}^{2}=-{e}^{2}<0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,1)=({f}^{″}(1,1))_{11}=e>0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $H(1,1)<0,$ the point $(1,1)$ is a saddle point by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351251 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350891 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351251 1069 "q-81167" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351251 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351251 1071 "Find and categorize all local the extrema of the function $f(x,y)={e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},$ and determine which of the following statements is true." 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351251 1072 #uuid "ee951228-2bde-5532-9c92-cd8f867b2b2e" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351251 1074 "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f′(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & 2y{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting $f′(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=0 \\\\ 2y{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=0\\,⟹\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n2y{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=0\\,⟹\\,y=0.\n$$\n\nHence, $(0,0)$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify this stationary point, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=(4{x}^{2}+2){e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=4xy{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=4xy{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=(4{y}^{2}+2){e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative  is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}(4{x}^{2}+2){e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & 4xy{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\ 4xy{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & (4{y}^{2}+2){e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(0,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,0)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(0,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(0,0)=det({f}^{″}(0,0))=2\\cdot 2-{0}^{2}=4>0.\n$$\n\nFinally, notice that \n\n$$\n\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(0,0)=({f}^{″}(0,0))_{11}=2>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second partial derivative test, we have that $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(0,0).$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351252 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350782 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351252 1069 "q-81171" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351252 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351252 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{(2x+y+1)}{x}\\,dx+dy,$ where $C$ is a portion of the parabola parametrized by $r(t)=t\\,i+({t}^{2}-1)\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2].$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351252 1072 #uuid "f750ea59-b3de-591d-8c26-90afaaf302f8" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351252 1074 "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=t,\\,y(t)={t}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-1)\\,j \\\\  & =i+2t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{2x+y+1}{x}\\,dx+dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{2x+y+1}{x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{2x+y+1}{x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{2t+{t}^{2}-1+1}{t}+2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2+t+2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2+3t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[2t+\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =[2(2)+\\frac{3}{2}(2)^{2}]-[0] \\\\  & =[4+6] \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351253 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350839 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351253 1069 "q-81173" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351253 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351253 1071 "If $U={1,2,3,…,10}$ is the universal set and $A={1,3,5,7,9},$ then the complement of $A$ is" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351253 1072 #uuid "85de247f-7895-5088-9c16-dac74b53044c" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351253 1074 "The complement of $A,$ denoted $\\overset{―}{A},$ consists of every element in the universal set $U$ that is not in the set $A.$\n\nThe elements of $U$ that are not in $A$ are $2,$ $4,$ $6,$ $8,$ and $10.$\n\nTherefore, the complement of $A$ is\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{A}={2,4,6,8,10}.\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351254 1068 #edb/ref 17592186351129 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351254 1069 "q-81176" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351254 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351254 1071 "If $U={p,q,r,s,t,u}$ is the universal set and $S={q,s,t},$ then the complement of $S$ is" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351254 1072 #uuid "46667089-6f65-5aeb-882c-6be39a19f756" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351254 1074 "The complement of $S,$ denoted $\\overset{―}{S},$ consists of every element in the universal set $U$ that is not in the set $S.$\n\nThe only elements of $U$ that are not in $S$ are $p,$ $r,$ and $u.$\n\nTherefore, the complement of $S$ is\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{S}={p,r,u}.\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351255 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350978 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351255 1069 "q-81177" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351255 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351255 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}y\\,dx+x\\,dy,$ where $C$ is a portion of the ellipse parametrized by $r(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{12},\\frac{\\pi }{4}].$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351255 1072 #uuid "fde6cd1e-82de-5878-af27-eda97f1dc77e" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351255 1074 "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y(t)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n.\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}y\\,dx+x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /4}y\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /4}(y\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /4}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /4}{cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t){|}_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =4-2 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351256 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350796 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351256 1069 "q-81180" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351256 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351256 1071 "If the path $C$ is a part of the parabola $y=\\pi {x}^{2}$ traversed from the point $A(1,\\pi )$ to point $B(2,4\\pi ),$ then ${\\int }_{C}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{y}{2{x}^{2}})\\,dx+3x\\,dy=$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351256 1072 #uuid "bbe13972-64bc-5d76-9613-e21318b0221e" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351256 1074 "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y=\\pi {x}^{2}.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=t\\,i+\\pi {t}^{2}\\,j,\\,t\\in [1,2]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=i+2\\pi t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{y}{2{x}^{2}})\\,dx+3x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{y}{2{x}^{2}})\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+3x\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{y}{2{x}^{2}})\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+3x\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi {t}^{2}}{2{t}^{2}})\\cdot 1+3t\\cdot 2\\pi t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+6\\pi {t}^{2}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}1+6\\pi {t}^{2}\\,dt \\\\  & =[t+2\\pi {t}^{3}]_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =[2+2\\pi (2)^{3}]-[1+2\\pi (1)^{3}] \\\\  & =[2+16\\pi ]-[1+2\\pi ] \\\\  & =1+14\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351257 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350811 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351257 1069 "q-81184" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351257 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351257 1071 "If $v=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\sqrt{5}\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351257 1072 #uuid "931d7f2e-4fc8-5dd3-95f9-e9f514cf8458" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351257 1074 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =\\sqrt{5}\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5}\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{5}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2-\\sqrt{5}\\text{i} & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2-\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(2-\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}){x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+(-2-\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{3}(2+\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3}(2+\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-3,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}2+\\sqrt{5}\\text{i} \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{-3}{2+\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}}=\\frac{-3(2-\\sqrt{5}\\text{i})}{9}=-\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\text{i}.$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351258 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350882 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351258 1069 "q-81185" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351258 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351258 1071 "If $v=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2-3\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351258 1072 #uuid "fbfe9a5f-3d3d-54aa-9e82-2b9e587c84ba" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351258 1074 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =2-3\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambd