efore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{3}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]=\\overset{^}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(x,\\overset{^}{x}) & =‖x-\\overset{^}{x}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{2})^{2}+({x}_{3}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{3})^{2}+({x}_{4}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{4})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(3-0)^{2}+(-3-(-5))^{2}+(-9-(-2))^{2}+(3-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{63} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351473 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350852 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351473 1069 "q-82948" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351473 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351473 1071 "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the acute angle between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal.   Round the answer to $2$ decimal places." 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351473 1072 #uuid "2d9ec92a-312d-5203-9041-ef41221167cc" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351473 1074 "Since the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{2\\cdot 1+6\\cdot (-1)+(-2)\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{1}=(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{2\\cdot 1+6\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x$ or any other non-zero vector that is parallel to ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ \n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x\\cdot {\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x}{‖x‖\\,‖{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 0+6\\cdot 6+(-2)\\cdot 0}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{6}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{6}^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{\\sqrt{44}\\sqrt{36}} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{2\\sqrt{11}\\cdot 6} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{11}}{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{3\\sqrt{11}}{11})≈{25.24}^{∘},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle." 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351474 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350781 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351474 1069 "q-82950" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351474 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351474 1071 "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the acute angle between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal." 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351474 1072 #uuid "149122b9-3f44-51a2-8102-80d5520846f1" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351474 1074 "Since the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{5\\cdot 2+1\\cdot (-2)+3\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 1}{{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{5\\cdot 3+1\\cdot 2+3\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-2)}{{3}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x$ or any other non-zero vector that is parallel to ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ \n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x\\cdot {\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x}{‖x‖\\,‖{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\cdot 5+1\\cdot 0+3\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-1)}{\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{\\sqrt{36}\\sqrt{27}} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{6\\cdot 3\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{6},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle." 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351475 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350883 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351475 1069 "q-82952" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351475 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351475 1071 "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the acute angle between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal." 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351475 1072 #uuid "57b827fd-22c8-5e6c-a1b5-310eea530f06" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351475 1074 "Since the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{5\\cdot 3+5\\cdot 0+2\\cdot (-2)+3\\cdot 1}{{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{5\\cdot 2+5\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 2+3\\cdot (-2)}{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x$ or any other non-zero vector that is parallel to ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ \n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x\\cdot {\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x}{‖x‖\\,‖{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\cdot 5+5\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 0+3\\cdot (-1)}{\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{5}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{3}^{2}}\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{\\sqrt{63}\\sqrt{27}} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{3\\sqrt{7}\\cdot 3\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{7})≈{49.11}^{∘},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle." 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351476 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350917 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351476 1069 "q-82955" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351476 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351476 1071 "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the acute angle between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal." 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351476 1072 #uuid "f098e9a9-c478-53e3-b6f8-cb3abb9f2b85" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351476 1074 "Since the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{3\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 1+(-3)\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{3}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{3\\cdot 2+4\\cdot (-1)+(-3)\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot 1}{{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x$ or any other non-zero vector that is parallel to ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ So, to make calculations easier, we can find the angle between the vectors\n\n$\\,$ $x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,2\\,{\\text{proj}}_{S}x=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].$\n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{3\\cdot 7+4\\cdot 1+(-3)\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot 5}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\sqrt{{7}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{5}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{38}{\\sqrt{38}\\sqrt{76}} \\\\  & =\\frac{38}{38\\cdot \\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{4},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle." 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351477 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350766 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351477 1069 "q-8296" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351477 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351477 1071 "Let \n$T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}5x+2y \\\\ 3y\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $T(kv)=kT(v)$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vector $v$ and real scalar $k$\n- $T({v}_{1}+{v}_{2})=T({v}_{1})+T({v}_{2})$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$\n- $T$ is a linear transformation" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351477 1072 #uuid "d21c33a8-a8c0-5b77-a079-4c787c111b5f" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351477 1074 "A linear transformation $T$ satisfies two properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}]=kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}], \\\\  & T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the first condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5(kx)+2(ky) \\\\ 3(ky)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}k(5x+2y) \\\\ k(3y)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =k[\\begin{aligned}5x+2y \\\\ 3y\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the second condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5({x}_{1}+{x}_{2})+2({y}_{1}+{y}_{2}) \\\\ 3({y}_{1}+{y}_{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{1}+2{y}_{1} \\\\ 3{y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{2}+2{y}_{2} \\\\ 3{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{T}$ is a linear transformation.\n\nAs a result, all three statements are true." 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351478 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350827 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351478 1069 "q-8297" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351478 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351478 1071 "Let \n$T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}3y \\\\ 2x\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $T(kv)=kT(v)$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vector $v$ and real scalar $k$\n- $T({v}_{1}+{v}_{2})=T({v}_{1})+T({v}_{2})$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$\n- $T$ is a linear transformation" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351478 1072 #uuid "5104f251-39bc-5c29-bbcf-96e73f6b8fe5" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351478 1074 "A linear transformation $T$ satisfies two properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}]=kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}], \\\\  & T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the first condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3ky \\\\ 2kx\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =k[\\begin{aligned}3y \\\\ 2x\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the sec