\\\\ 0 & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 1+{C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{1} & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we get the particular solution in implicit form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y) & =\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the above equation as\n\n$$\n3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y)=2(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)).\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351488 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350942 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351488 1069 "q-8311" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351488 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351488 1071 "Find the specific solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4=0$ given that $y(-1)=4.$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351488 1072 #uuid "8d35cfdf-da63-5e6d-990c-a2e5c83f1725" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351488 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation to give\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-4.\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (-4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\,\\text{d}y & =\\int (-4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =-4x+C\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo our general solution is $y=-4x+C.$ To find a particular solution, we substitute the condition $y(-1)=4$ into our solution and obtain a value for $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =-4\\cdot (-1)+C \\\\ 4 & =4+C \\\\ 0 & =C\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\ny=-4x\\,.\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351489 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350867 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351489 1069 "q-83161" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351489 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351489 1071 "Given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}+x+3}{({x}^{2}+4)(x-1)}=\\frac{Ax+1}{{x}^{2}+4}+\\frac{1}{x-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant to be found, calculate $\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+x+3}{({x}^{2}+4)(x-1)}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351489 1072 #uuid "b3d16f22-84af-5aa2-bf4e-f306446b007f" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351489 1074 "First, we find the constant $A.$ Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $({x}^{2}+4)(x-1)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+x+3 & =(Ax+1)(x-1)+{x}^{2}+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2$  into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{2}+2+3 & =(A(2)+1)(2-1)+{2}^{2}+4 \\\\ 9 & =2A+1+8 \\\\ 9 & =2A+9 \\\\ A & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe now integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+x+3}{({x}^{2}+4)(x-1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{0x+1}{{x}^{2}+4}+\\frac{1}{x-1})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first integral, we use the fact that $\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{a}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{a}),$ which results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|.\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+x+3}{({x}^{2}+4)(x-1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351490 1068 #edb/ref 17592186351068 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351490 1069 "q-83179" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351490 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351490 1071 "Express the Maclaurin series expansion for $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x),$ given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)=x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}+⋯,\\,x\\in (-1,1]\n$$\n\nusing sigma notation." 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351490 1072 #uuid "32d39774-c068-5b50-8421-a955ffada627" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351490 1074 "The first four terms of the series are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =x \\\\ {a}_{2} & =-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2} \\\\ {a}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3} \\\\ {a}_{3} & =-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we can express these terms using a common format, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =\\frac{(-1)^{2}{x}^{1}}{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} & =\\frac{(-1)^{3}{x}^{2}}{2} \\\\ {a}_{3} & =\\frac{(-1)^{4}{x}^{3}}{3} \\\\ {a}_{4} & =\\frac{(-1)^{5}{x}^{4}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe deduce that the general formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{x}^{n}}{n}\\,.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{x}^{n}}{n}.\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351491 1068 #edb/ref 17592186351049 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351491 1069 "q-83181" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351491 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351491 1071 "Express the Maclaurin series expansion for $\\frac{1}{(1-x)^{2}},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{(1-x)^{2}}=1+2x+3{x}^{2}+4{x}^{3}+⋯\\,,\\,x\\in (-1,1)\n$$\n\nusing sigma notation." 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351491 1072 #uuid "618eef5f-9c9f-578d-9365-5718513b1123" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351491 1074 "The first four terms of the series are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =1 \\\\ {a}_{1} & =2x \\\\ {a}_{2} & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ {a}_{3} & =4{x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we can express these terms using a common format, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & ={x}^{0} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =(1+1){x}^{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} & =(2+1){x}^{2} \\\\ {a}_{3} & =(3+1){x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe deduce that the general formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=(n+1){x}^{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{(1-x)^{2}}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(n+1){x}^{n}\\,.\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351492 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350787 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351492 1069 "q-83185" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351492 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351492 1071 "Find the first four non-zero terms in the Taylor expansion of $\\frac{1}{x+3}$ about the point $x=-2.$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351492 1072 #uuid "4890c809-0162-5587-a5b1-c84e2c1d1122" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351492 1074 "We use Taylor's theorem in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(a)}{3!}(x-a)^{3}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=\\frac{1}{x+3}$ and $a=-2.$ \n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{x+3}=(x+3)^{-1} \\\\ f(-2) & =(-2+3)^{-1}=1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{(x+3)^{2}}=-(x+3)^{-2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(-2) & =-(-2+3)^{-2}=-1 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{2}{(x+3)^{3}}=2(x+3)^{-3} \\\\ {f}^{″}(-2) & =2(-2+3)^{-3}=2 \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =-\\frac{6}{(x+3)^{4}}=-6(x+3)^{-4} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(-2) & =-6(-2+3)^{-4}=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x+3} & =1+(-1)(x+2)+\\frac{2}{2!}(x+2)^{2}+\\frac{(-6)}{3!}(x+2)^{3}+⋯ \\\\  & =1-(x+2)+(x+2)^{2}-(x+2)^{3}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351493 1068 #edb/ref 17592186351085 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351493 1069 "q-83187" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351493 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351493 1071 "Find the first three non-zero terms in the Taylor expansion of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ centered about the point $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351493 1072 #uuid "5f218cc7-60a5-53f7-bb6e-74dc39f98842" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351493 1074 "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(a)}{3!}(x-a)^{3}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and $a=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ f(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=-1 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0 \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{‴}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1 \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0 \\\\ {f}^{(5)}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{(5)}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0+(-1)(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{0}{2!}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2} \\\\  & +\\frac{1}{3!}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{3}+\\frac{0}{4!}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{4}+\\frac{(-1)}{5!}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{5}+⋯ \\\\  & =-(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{1}{6}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{3}-\\frac{1}{120}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{5}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351494 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350914 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351494 1069 "q-83193" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351494 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351494 1071 "Find the first three non-zero terms in the Taylor expansion of $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)$ about the point $x=1.$" 1319413