that $\\text{tr}(A)=-14$ and $\\text{det}(A)=-36.$ Then, ${A}^{2}=$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351511 1072 #uuid "1eff7632-93b0-5245-bbd8-e6ffe12d224e" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351511 1074 "The characteristic polynomial of $A$ is given by \n\n$$\np(\\lambda )={\\lambda }^{2}-\\text{tr}(A)\\lambda +\\text{det}(A).\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(A) & =O \\\\ {A}^{2}-\\text{tr}(A)A+\\text{det}(A)I & =O \\\\ {A}^{2} & =\\text{tr}(A)A-\\text{det}(A)I \\\\ {A}^{2} & =-14A+36I.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351512 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350995 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351512 1069 "q-83401" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351512 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351512 1071 "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨{e}^{t},\\,{t}^{3},\\,2t⟩$ for $t\\ge 0$, use the cross product curvature formula to compute the curvature at the point $P(1,0,0).$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351512 1072 #uuid "0a52c325-2b2d-52d8-8149-5e02da5ab5bb" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351512 1074 "The curvature $κ(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to the point $P(1,0,0)$ on the given curve. We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨1,0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨{e}^{t},\\,{t}^{3},\\,2t⟩ & =⟨1,0,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the respective components gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t}=1 \\\\ {t}^{3}=0 \\\\ 2t=0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equality, $t=0.$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(1,0,0)$ is at the tip of $r(0).$ \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$, ${r}^{′}(0),$  and $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t},\\,3{t}^{2},\\,2⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(0) & =⟨1,0,2⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}}=\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find ${r}^{″}(t)$ and ${r}^{″}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t},\\,6t,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(0) & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross-product and its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0) & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =2j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(0) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(0){‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(\\sqrt{5})^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351513 1068 #edb/ref 17592186351024 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351513 1069 "q-83402" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351513 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351513 1071 "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨2t,\\,{t}^{2},\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡t⟩$ for $t>0$, use the cross product curvature formula to compute the curvature at the point $P(2,1,0).$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351513 1072 #uuid "62f8ada8-0edf-581d-85ea-b6a85fd96890" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351513 1074 "The curvature $κ(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to the point $P(2,1,0)$ on the given curve. We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨2,1,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨2t,\\,{t}^{2},\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡t⟩ & =⟨2,1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the respective components gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2t=2 \\\\ {t}^{2}=1 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡t=0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equality, $t=1.$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(2,1,0)$ is at the tip of $r(1).$ \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$, ${r}^{′}(1),$  and $‖{r}^{′}(1)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,2t,\\,\\frac{1}{t}⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(1) & =⟨2,2,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(1)‖ & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find ${r}^{″}(t)$ and ${r}^{″}(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2,\\,-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(1) & =⟨0,2,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross-product and its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(1)\\times {r}^{″}(1) & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =-4i+2j+4k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(1)\\times {r}^{″}(1)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+4+16} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(1) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(1)\\times {r}^{″}(1)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(1){‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{{3}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351514 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350820 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351514 1069 "q-83439" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351514 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351514 1071 "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,$ use the cross product curvature formula to find the curvature as a function of $t.$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351514 1072 #uuid "4c4431df-a097-5304-afd8-3847a2139331" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351514 1074 "The curvature $κ(t)$ is given by:\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}\\,\\,\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t+2{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the second derivative ${r}^{″}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross-product and its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t) & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\times ⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}i-\\sqrt{2}j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the magnitude of the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\sqrt{2})}^{2}+{(-\\sqrt{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2+2} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}=\\frac{2}{(\\sqrt{2})^{3}}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351515 1068 #edb/ref 17592186351070 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351515 1069 "q-83442" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351515 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351515 1071 "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+1$ on the closed region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1}.$ You are given that the function has a critical point at $(0,0)\\in D.$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351515 1072 #uuid "8a46a5d2-f50e-55ed-a8fd-136bbe52d472" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351515 1074 "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ that lie inside the region $D.$ \n\nWe have been given that $f$ has a critical point at \n\n$\\,$ $(0,0)$ \n\nwhich belongs to the interior of the region $D.$ So, we include this point in the list of candidates for the extrema. \n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$ This gives  \n\n$$\nr=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t+1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,2\\pi ),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+1)^{′} & =0 \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation gives the solutions $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\pi ,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\in (0,2\\pi ).$\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can possibly give rise to extreme values are $t=0,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\pi ,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ Evaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain four candidate points: \n\n$$\n(1,0),\\,(0,1),\\,(-1,0),\\,(0,-1)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values.\n\nLet's compare the values of $f(x,y)$ evaluated at each of the points mentioned above. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =(0)^{2}-(0)^{2}+1 \\\\  & =0-0+1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ f(1,0) & =(1)^{2}-(0)^{2}+1 \\\\  & =1-0+1 \\\\  & =2 \\\\ f(0,1) & =(0)^{2}-(1)^{2}+1 \\\\  & =0-1+1 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ f(-1,0) & =(-1)^{2}-(0)^{2}+1 \\\\  & =1-0+1 \\\\  & =2 \\\\ f(0,-1) & =(0)^{2}-(-1)^{2}+1 \\\\  & =0-1+1 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $0$ at the point $(0,-1),$ and  $(0,1),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $2$ at the point $(-1,0),$ and $(1,0).$" 13194139533430 17592186045585 true] [17592186351516 